window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Everything the instructor explicitly said would be tested or heavily hinted. A ★ means it was said out loud."]},{"s":"Week 1▾","a":"week-1","k":["Week 1▾","Topic Name","★ A short, quick-reference bullet (about 20 words).","An unstarred bullet keeps its attribution: she hinted this would show up.","Not Tested","Record every \"you don't need to memorize this\" the moment it's said."]}]},{"f":"week1-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 1","Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome","Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction","Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology","Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 1: the four topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: myocarditis, pericarditis and cardiac tamponade (Part 1). Topic 2: acute coronary syndrome …"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade▾","a":"part-1-myocarditis-pericarditis-cardiac-tamponade","k":["Part 1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade▾","Topic 1, the first lecture of NUR 406: myocarditis, then the pericardium, how inflammation leads to pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade, the bedside signs, pulsus paradoxus, and treatment.","Myocarditis","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used.","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood.","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","Pulsus Paradoxus","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added.","Pericarditis on the ECG","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","Treating Pericarditis","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly."]},{"s":"Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","a":"part-2a-acute-coronary-syndrome","k":["Part 2A — Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","Topic 2, first half: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, and how ACS differs from stable angina.","What ACS Is","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible.","The ACS Spectrum","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section).","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for.","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on.","Why Plaque Ruptures","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area.","Unstable Angina","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER.","Stable Angina vs ACS","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse."]},{"s":"Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"part-2b-myocardial-infarction","k":["Part 2B — Myocardial Infarction▾","Topic 2, second half: the signs of MI, what an acute MI is, what happens to heart tissue over time, the zones of damage, STEMI vs NSTEMI, and the LAD.","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest.","Acute Myocardial Infarction","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible.","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes.","Zones of Damage","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…"]},{"s":"Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology▾","a":"part-3-acute-mi-pharmacology","k":["Part 3 — Acute MI Pharmacology▾","Topic 3, the second part of the same ACS PowerPoint: the initial drugs, fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, nitroglycerin safety, reperfusion injury, and the key takeaways. Diagnostics and surgical reperfusion are covered in med-surg, not …","Initial Drug Treatment","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size).","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated.","Nitroglycerin Safety","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it.","Reperfusion Injury","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation.","Key Takeaways","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection▾","a":"part-4-aneurysms-aortic-dissection","k":["Part 4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection▾","Topic 4: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, the types by location and shape, assessing a AAA, and the two big complications, rupture and aortic dissection.","What an Aneurysm Is","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component","Types of Aneurysms","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil.","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\"","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death.","Managing Aortic Dissection","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1. Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Myocarditis is mostly viral (Coxsackie A and B) and can disrupt conduction. Normal pericardial fluid is 15–50 mL; about 200 mL compresses the heart into cardiac tamponade, a medical emergen…","2A. Acute Coronary Syndrome — ACS = unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI; time is tissue. Prinzmetal's is vasospasm, often at rest, treated with nitrates. Unstable plaque: big lipid core, thin fibrous cap, inflammation (CRP). First-time chest pai…","2B. Myocardial Infarction — MI is irreversible ischemic necrosis; ECG changes stay and troponin trends up. Myocardial cells can't store ATP: contraction drops in 1–2 minutes, irreversible injury from 30 minutes to 4 hours, so reperfuse with…","3. Acute MI Pharmacology — Take vital signs before every drug. Aspirin: chew the first dose; decreases mortality in STEMI. Morphine: watch respiratory depression and hypotension; may inhibit antiplatelets. Nitroglycerin: reduces preload and…","4. Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — Primarily from hypertension; turbulent flow, a bruit, rupture risk. Saccular (one side, wide neck), berry (small neck, circle of Willis; drain, clip or coil), fusiform (whole circumference, monitored), fal…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the five parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week1-pericardial-disease.html","t":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","secs":[{"s":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Myocarditis","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","Pulsus Paradoxus","Pericarditis on the ECG","Treating Pericarditis","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first lecture of NUR 406, a short voiceover on pericardial disease. It covers myocarditis (inflammation of the heart muscle), then the pericardium itself: how inflammation of the pericardium leads to fluid buildup, pericardial e…"]},{"s":"Myocarditis▾","a":"myocarditis","k":["Myocarditis▾","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used."]},{"s":"The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","a":"the-pericardium-pericardial-fluid","k":["The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood."]},{"s":"Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","a":"acute-pericarditis-vs-cardiac-tamponade","k":["Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade."]},{"s":"Pulsus Paradoxus▾","a":"pulsus-paradoxus","k":["Pulsus Paradoxus▾","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added."]},{"s":"Pericarditis on the ECG▾","a":"pericarditis-on-the-ecg","k":["Pericarditis on the ECG▾","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","ST elevation in MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction. Compare the two if needed"]},{"s":"Treating Pericarditis▾","a":"treating-pericarditis","k":["Treating Pericarditis▾","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly.","Aspirin for acute MI is a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acs-mi.html","t":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","secs":[{"s":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","What ACS Is","The ACS Spectrum","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Why Plaque Ruptures","Unstable Angina","Stable Angina vs ACS","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","Acute Myocardial Infarction","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Zones of Damage","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the ACS voiceover: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, how ACS differs from stable angina, the signs of MI, what happens to heart ti…"]},{"s":"What ACS Is▾","a":"what-acs-is","k":["What ACS Is▾","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible."]},{"s":"The ACS Spectrum▾","a":"the-acs-spectrum","k":["The ACS Spectrum▾","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section)."]},{"s":"Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","a":"prinzmetal-s-variant-or-vasospastic-angina","k":["Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for."]},{"s":"What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","a":"what-makes-plaque-unstable","k":["What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on."]},{"s":"Why Plaque Ruptures▾","a":"why-plaque-ruptures","k":["Why Plaque Ruptures▾","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area."]},{"s":"Unstable Angina▾","a":"unstable-angina","k":["Unstable Angina▾","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER."]},{"s":"Stable Angina vs ACS▾","a":"stable-angina-vs-acs","k":["Stable Angina vs ACS▾","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse."]},{"s":"Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"signs-symptoms-of-myocardial-infarction","k":["Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest."]},{"s":"Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"acute-myocardial-infarction","k":["Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible."]},{"s":"Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","a":"ischemia-infarction-atp","k":["Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes."]},{"s":"Zones of Damage▾","a":"zones-of-damage","k":["Zones of Damage▾","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…"]},{"s":"STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","a":"stemi-vs-nstemi","k":["STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","Pericarditis can also show ST elevation and can look like an MI. See Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade"]},{"s":"The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","a":"the-lad-the-widow-maker","k":["The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acute-mi-pharm.html","t":"Acute MI Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Acute MI Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Initial Drug Treatment","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","Nitroglycerin Safety","Reperfusion Injury","Key Takeaways","Self-Test Flashcards","The second, shorter part of the ACS voiceover. It covers the initial drug treatment of an acute MI (oxygen, morphine, aspirin, nitroglycerin, beta blockers and thrombolytics), fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, reperfusion injury, and the…","The pathophysiology of ACS and MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction"]},{"s":"Initial Drug Treatment▾","a":"initial-drug-treatment","k":["Initial Drug Treatment▾","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size)."]},{"s":"Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","a":"fibrinolytic-therapy-alteplase-tpa","k":["Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated."]},{"s":"Nitroglycerin Safety▾","a":"nitroglycerin-safety","k":["Nitroglycerin Safety▾","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it."]},{"s":"Reperfusion Injury▾","a":"reperfusion-injury","k":["Reperfusion Injury▾","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation."]},{"s":"Key Takeaways▾","a":"key-takeaways","k":["Key Takeaways▾","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-aneurysms.html","t":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","secs":[{"s":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","What an Aneurysm Is","Types of Aneurysms","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Managing Aortic Dissection","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the aneurysm voiceover: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, risk factors, the types by location and by shape (saccular, berry, fusiform and false aneurysms), assessing an abdominal aortic aneurysm, and the two big compli…"]},{"s":"What an Aneurysm Is▾","a":"what-an-aneurysm-is","k":["What an Aneurysm Is▾","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component"]},{"s":"Types of Aneurysms▾","a":"types-of-aneurysms","k":["Types of Aneurysms▾","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil."]},{"s":"Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","a":"abdominal-aortic-aneurysm-aaa","k":["Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …"]},{"s":"Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","a":"complications-rupture-aortic-dissection","k":["Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\""]},{"s":"Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","a":"aneurysm-vs-aortic-dissection","k":["Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death."]},{"s":"Managing Aortic Dissection▾","a":"managing-aortic-dissection","k":["Managing Aortic Dissection▾","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment.","Beta blockers and nitroglycerin in acute MI are a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 1","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics","The Four Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 1's four topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week …"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-four-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","Four pages are easier to hold in your head when you see what each lecture is really about. Topics 2 and 3 are the two halves of one PowerPoint.","The Heart Muscle and Its Sac: Inflammation — Topic 1, Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade (all of it) — The first lecture of 406. Inflammation of the myocardium (myocarditis, usually viral) can range from mild to heart failure and…","The Coronary Arteries: Blockage — Topic 2, Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction (the ACS sections first, then the MI sections) — The longest lecture. Blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised, and the si…","Treating the Blockage — Topic 3, Acute MI Pharmacology (all of it) — The second, shorter half of the ACS PowerPoint. Each drug has a job: oxygen raises delivery to the ischemic muscle, morphine eases pain, aspirin keeps the thrombus from gr…","The Artery Wall: Weakening and Tearing — Topic 4, Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection (all of it) — Back out to the vessels. Uncontrolled hypertension weakens the arterial wall until it bulges (an aneurysm), and a weakened wall can tear (dissecti…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order, and read Topic 2 before Topic 3: the pharmacology lecture assumes the plaque, clot and \"time is tissue\" ideas from ACS."]},{"s":"The Four Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-four-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Four Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Myocarditis is mostly viral (Coxsackie A and B), treated with antivirals and anti-inflammatories depending on severity. Pericarditis brings sharp chest pain that worsens with deep breathin…","2 — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction — ACS is unstable angina, NSTEMI and STEMI, and time is tissue. Unstable plaque (big lipid core, thin cap, inflammation) ruptures under sympathetic stress and a thrombus forms. Unstable an…","3 — Acute MI Pharmacology — Oxygen, morphine (now used more cautiously), chewed aspirin, nitroglycerin and beta blockers, all after checking vital signs. Aspirin decreases mortality; nitroglycerin does not. PCI is first choice for STEMI; al…","4 — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — Aneurysms come primarily from hypertension and atherosclerosis, cause turbulent flow and a bruit, and are most common in the aorta and cerebral arteries. Saccular, berry, fusiform and false aneurysms diff…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","ST elevation on the ECG — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"STEMI vs NSTEMI\" (elevation in STEMI; normal or slight depression in NSTEMI) — The same page's \"Prinzmetal's Angina\" section (it may show ST elevation, which return…","Aspirin — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" (chew the first dose; suppresses platelet aggregation; decreases mortality in STEMI) — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Treating Pericarditis,\" where aspirin may be se…","Nitrates and nitroglycerin — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" and \"Nitroglycerin Safety\" (reduces preload and afterload; no sildenafil) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: nitrates relax the spasm in Prinzmetal…","Beta blockers — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Initial Drug Treatment\" (lower heart rate and contractility, so lower oxygen demand) — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: \"Managing Aortic Dissection,\" to decrease heart rate and blood pressure and preven…","Blood pressure and force on the vessel wall — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: \"What an Aneurysm Is\" (uncontrolled hypertension weakens the arterial wall) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"Why Plaque Ruptures\" (sympathetic s…","Atherosclerosis — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"The ACS Spectrum\" and \"What Makes Plaque Unstable\" — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: a primary driver in \"Risk Factors,\" with fusiform aneurysms related to diffuse sclerotic…","Inflammation — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade (inflammation of the myocardium and of the pericardium) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"What Makes Plaque Unstable,\" where inflammation (measured by CRP) makes…","Conduction problems and dysrhythmias — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Myocarditis\" (inflammation disrupts conduction) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: Prinzmetal's can cause serious dysrhythmias, and scar …","Heart failure — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Myocarditis\" (mild all the way up to heart failure) — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Reperfusion Injury,\" where stunned myocardium can cause heart failure.","Poor perfusion and falling blood pressure — Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade: \"Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade\" (decreased cardiac output: hypotension, confusion, dizziness, JVD) — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial I…","Bleeding — Acute MI Pharmacology: \"Fibrinolytic Therapy\" (bleeding is the main adverse effect; no thrombolytic after a recent hemorrhagic stroke) — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: a ruptured AAA bleeds out very quickly; late dissection bleed…","Dye studies and angiography — Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection: saccular aneurysms on an angiogram, berry aneurysms diagnosed by angiography, dissection by CT or MRA — Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction: \"What Makes Plaque Unstabl…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the four pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any …","Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade — Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency: the fluid has to be removed quickly (pericardiocentesis), because the chambers can't stretch and fill. Watch for decreased cardiac output: hypotension…","Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction — First-time chest pain is unstable angina until proven otherwise. Unstable angina is a medical emergency: call 911, never drive to the ER. Take an impending sense of doom very seriously. Time…","Acute MI Pharmacology — Take heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before giving any of these drugs. Morphine: respiratory depression and hypotension. Nitroglycerin: never with sildenafil (profound hypotension); IV only if blood…","Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection — No deep palpation of a suspected AAA (rupture risk); light palpation only. A ruptured AAA bleeds out very quickly. Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency where minutes matter; without intervention it progr…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"The emergency is when blood can't get where it needs to go.\" Tamponade stops the heart from filling, a complete clot in a STEMI stops blood reaching the heart muscle, and a rupture or dissection lets blood escape the vessel. The lectures c…","\"The quality of the pain is a clue.\" Pericarditis: sharp and worse with deep breathing. ACS: longer, more severe, an elephant on the chest, no relief with nitroglycerin. Aortic dissection: sudden, severe and tearing. And some don't hurt at …","\"Look-alikes on the ECG.\" Pericarditis can show ST elevation like an MI; Prinzmetal's angina can show ST elevation that returns to normal; unstable angina's ECG changes return to normal while an MI's stay. The ECG alone isn't the whole stor…","\"Check before you give.\" The MI drugs can push an unstable patient over the edge, so vital signs come first, and several drugs carry a condition: morphine if they can tolerate it, IV nitroglycerin if blood pressure tolerates it, beta blocke…","\"Don't let the patient wait.\" Hesitating or delaying care is a big reason for MI deaths, while treated patients survive about 90 to 95% of the time. Teach patients to call 911 for unstable angina and to seek care if they suspect an MI: bett…"]}]},{"f":"week2-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 2","Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids","Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH","Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders","Part 2B — ABG Interpretation","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 2: the two topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: acid-base balance (Parts 1A–1B). Topic 2: ABGs and ABG interpretation (Parts 2A–2B). It inte…"]},{"s":"Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids▾","a":"part-1a-ph-hydrogen-acids","k":["Part 1A — pH, Hydrogen & Acids▾","Topic 1, first voiceover (Acid base 1): what pH is, why hydrogen matters, normal serum pH and how pH varies in the body, what pH affects (including oxygen delivery), the survivable limits, and volatile vs non-volatile acids.","What pH Is","pH, acids and bases","pH is the potential, or the power, of a hydrogen ion. It is a scale that identifies whether a solution is more acidic or more alkaline, based on the hydrogen ions present.","The bottom of the scale is more acidic, the middle is neutral, and the higher the number, the more basic (alkaline).","pH is inversely related to hydrogen ion concentration: when hydrogen ions go up, pH goes down (more acidic); when hydrogen ions go down, pH goes up (more basic).","The scale is logarithmic, not linear: each number is 10 times more acidic or alkaline than the one before it. A pH of 5 is 10 times more acidic than a pH of 6; going from 7 to 6 is a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","An acid can donate (release) hydrogen ions. A base can accept hydrogen ions; bases have fewer hydrogen ions and give up a hydroxide ion (OH⁻).","A strong acid completely dissociates in solution (gives up all its hydrogen ions). A weak acid only partially dissociates.","Where Common Things Fall on the Scale","Acidic side (lower numbers)","Milk, coffee, tomato, lemon","Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid)","Battery acid","Neutral and alkaline side","Water: 7, neutral","Blood: on the alkaline side; the slide shows it at 8, but it is really a little lower than 8","Baking soda, stomach tablets (like Alka-Seltzer), soap","Drain cleaner: 14","Why Hydrogen Matters","What hydrogen does in the body","Helps maintain cell membranes.","Helps with enzymatic activity.","Is a component of water and keeps the body hydrated.","Helps with energy production.","Is a component of the sugars, proteins, starches and fats we consume.","The body works to stay in homeostasis and functions best within certain ranges, like a heart rate of 60 to 100 or sodium of 135 to 145. pH is one of those ranges.","Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body","Normal serum (blood) pH: 7.35 to 7.45. Most of the body's enzymes function within that very narrow range.","pH isn't the same everywhere. The stomach is the most acidic place in the body, with hydrochloric acid at a pH more like 1. The stomach is uniquely designed for it: mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate at the cell surface to protect t…","Serum (blood) — 7.35 to 7.45","Saliva — 6.5 to 7.5","Stomach — As low as 1.5 on the GI chart (about 1 in the narration), then pH increases as contents move through the GI tract. The GI tract's differences aid digestion.","Skeletal muscle — 6.9 to 7.2","Bone — 7.4","Liver — 7.2","Pleural fluid — 7.6","What pH Affects","Acid-base balance affects","Electrolytes: sodium, potassium in particular, and chloride.","Hormones.","Oxygen transport and delivery, which is greatly affected.","Acidic environment","Hemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily at the tissue.","Alkalotic environment","Hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and doesn't give it to the tissue, which can result in inadequate tissue oxygenation.","The slide shows this on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: the relationship between oxygen saturation of the blood and oxygen partial pressure, which shifts as the blood becomes acidotic or alkalotic.","Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits","Below 7.35 — Acidosis.","7.35 to 7.45 — Normal.","Above 7.45 — Alkalosis.","Below 6.8 or above 7.8 — Incompatible with life, because of pH's profound effects on cellular metabolism, enzyme activity and oxygen delivery.","The body can function outside the normal range for a short time, but because pH affects so much (neurologic, respiratory, GI and cardiovascular function), the goal is to get back to normal as quickly as possible.","Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids","Volatile acids","Can be eliminated as carbon dioxide (CO2) gas, by the lungs (pulmonary ventilation).","The only volatile acid in the body is carbonic acid (H2CO3).","Carbonic acid is a weak acid: it does not easily release its hydrogen ion.","In the presence of the enzyme carbonic anhydrase, it readily dissociates into CO2 and water.","Non-volatile acids","Can be eliminated only by the kidneys.","Most are strong acids: they readily release their hydrogen ions.","Examples: lactic, phosphoric, sulfuric, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid (table below).","Lactic acid — A byproduct of anaerobic metabolism (metabolism without oxygen). A little different: it is metabolized by the liver and kidneys, and once oxygen is reestablished to the tissue it can be reconverted and used for energy again.","Phosphoric acid — Made from phosphorus; works with calcium to help with strong bones, and supports kidney function.","Sulfuric acid — Results from protein metabolism.","Acetoacetic acid — Produced by the liver.","Beta-hydroxybutyric acid — Increases with exercise, calorie restriction and fasting. Its measurement is used as a diagnostic for people in DKA (ketoacidosis).","Why the Body Leans Acidic","At baseline, the body produces more acids than bases: from the food we eat and the drinks we consume, from the metabolism of lipids and proteins, and from cellular metabolism, whose waste product is CO2.","We want to avoid alkalosis, especially if a person is ill, because hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and tissue oxygenation suffers. An alkaline pH is also really difficult to correct."]},{"s":"Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH▾","a":"part-1b-how-the-body-defends-its-ph","k":["Part 1B — How the Body Defends Its pH▾","Topic 1, second voiceover (Acid base 2): the three lines of defense, the buffer systems, the carbonic acid equation, cellular compensation and potassium, respiratory and renal control, and compensation.","The Three Lines of Defense","1. Chemical buffer systems — Almost instantaneously (seconds) — Primarily intracellular. Chemicals combine with acid or base to resist a change in pH. Makes only small changes; a relatively short, not sustainable fix.","2. Respiratory system — Several minutes to hours — Changes the rate or depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO2. The lungs are the primary controller of the carbonic acid supply.","3. Renal (kidney) control — Several hours to days — The long-term regulator. Excretes hydrogen ions and retains or excretes bicarb. The kidneys are the primary controller of bicarb (the base).","The lungs and kidneys work together. The lungs respond relatively quickly; the kidneys need more time, but their mechanisms are more long-term.","Buffer Systems","Buffers are chemicals that combine with an acid or base to change the pH and bring it back to normal. They can release or accept a hydrogen ion, and they work almost instantaneously, but they make small, short-term corrections only.","Bicarbonate–carbonic acid — The main buffer outside the cells (extracellular fluid) — CO2 from cell metabolism combines with water to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase breaks down in the lungs and kidneys (see the equation below)…","Phosphate — The main intracellular buffer — Picks up a hydrogen ion inside the cell to balance the pH inside the cell. It can't be measured. You don't need to memorize its equation.","Protein — Primarily intracellular (most proteins are in cells) — Most proteins can buffer. Carboxyl groups act as weak acids and release hydrogen ions; amino groups act as weak bases and accept them. When pH increases, proteins release hydr…","Hemoglobin — A protein buffer in the blood (some call it a fourth buffer system) — As pH increases, hemoglobin loses hydrogen ions, and the reverse when pH decreases. It also binds CO2 to form carbaminohemoglobin, which carries CO2 to the l…","The Carbonic Acid Equation","CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 (carbonic acid) ⇌ H⁺ + HCO3⁻ (bicarb). The instructor: \"You really want to be familiar and comfortable with the equation and know this backwards and forwards.\"","Lungs — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into CO2 and water, and the lungs exhale the CO2.","Kidneys — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into hydrogen ions and bicarb, and the kidneys excrete the hydrogen ions (and can generate bicarb).","The equation goes both directions: carbonic acid can break down into CO2 and water or form from them, and the same on the bicarb and hydrogen side.","A patient retaining CO2, like a COPD patient, pushes the equation in the other direction and increases bicarb.","CO2 also rises with respiratory depression, such as narcotic use or an overdose: the respiratory rate is so low the lungs can't remove the CO2, which changes the pH.","When a patient is acidotic and not compensating through the respiratory system, sodium bicarb is often given as a quick fix to raise the pH. This happens a lot in the ICU.","Cellular Compensation & Potassium","Cells like a neutral electrical balance. Hydrogen and potassium are both positively charged, and potassium is found primarily inside the cell.","In acidosis — Hydrogen ions move into the cell to balance the pH, and potassium moves out of the cell, which can raise blood potassium.","With kidney dysfunction — The kidneys can't get rid of the excess potassium: hyperkalemia, which can cause dysrhythmias.","When pH returns to normal — Hydrogen ions move back into the blood and potassium moves back into the cells.","With working kidneys — The kidneys will already have excreted the excess potassium, so this can lead to hypokalemia (to be revisited with DKA later in the course).","Cellular compensation is a temporary correction of the pH, not a permanent solution.","Respiratory Control of pH","The body produces CO2 continuously, which forms carbonic acid; in the lungs it breaks back down into water and CO2, and the excess CO2 is exhaled.","This works only on volatile acids, not on non-volatile acids like lactic acid.","The lungs adjust serum pH by changing the rate or depth of breathing. The brain and arterial receptors are stimulated by the pH change.","On mechanical ventilation in the ICU, these adjustments can be made through the ventilator settings.","pH too low (acidosis)","Breathing rate or depth increases → more CO2 blown off → less carbonic acid → pH rises.","pH too high (alkalosis)","Breathing rate or depth decreases → CO2 retained → more carbonic acid → pH falls.","Renal Control of pH","The kidneys are the last to alter pH and are the long-term regulators. They can eliminate large amounts of acid by getting rid of hydrogen ions, using an active transport mechanism.","They can also excrete bicarb (a base) if the pH is too high, or retain bicarb if the pH is too low.","Example: in a COPD patient retaining CO2, over time the kidneys compensate by holding on to more bicarb, so this patient has higher CO2 and higher bicarb to balance the serum pH.","Kidney function is extremely important: in renal failure, patients can no longer maintain a normal pH and often take bicarb daily.","Acidosis — Increased — Increased — Raises pH back to normal","Alkalosis — Decreased — Decreased — Lowers pH back to normal","The kidneys handle acidosis better than alkalosis. It is harder for the kidneys to excrete bicarb than to hold on to it.","Compensation","If one side of the equation is the problem, the body pulls toward the other side to rebalance. That movement is compensation: the body's response to an acid-base imbalance, aimed at bringing the pH back to normal. It is evaluated with arter…","Underlying problem is metabolic","The respiratory system compensates.","Underlying problem is respiratory","The renal system compensates, bringing about metabolic changes (metabolic compensation).","Uncompensated — Nothing has changed in response to a pH outside normal limits.","Partial compensation — Bicarb and CO2 levels have changed, but the pH is still outside normal limits.","Complete compensation — Bicarb or CO2 levels are abnormal, but the pH is back within normal limits.","What an ABG Measures","The lecture ends by previewing the ABG: pH, CO2 (PaCO2), PO2, HCO3 (bicarb) and O2 saturation. \"You do need to know these numbers.\" They are taught in the next lecture (Part 2A)."]},{"s":"Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders▾","a":"part-2a-abgs-the-four-acid-base-disorders","k":["Part 2A — ABGs & the Four Acid-Base Disorders▾","Topic 2, first voiceover (ABGs): why ABGs are drawn, the normal values, the key terms, ROME, and each of the four disorders with its ABG pattern, causes, signs and treatment.","Why We Draw ABGs","Purpose of an arterial blood gas","To assess acid-base status, determine the adequacy of oxygenation and ventilation, and decide which treatment would be best.","Arterial blood gives the best representation of the pH and PaCO2 in the body.","A venous blood gas (VBG) can be drawn, but the measurements differ: the CO2 is a bit higher (it's venous blood), which changes the pH measurement. If an arterial draw isn't possible, a VBG can gauge where things stand, but for critically il…","Normal ABG Values","\"You do need to know these numbers.\" pH 7.35–7.45 · PaCO2 35–45 · HCO3 24–29.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — The balance of hydrogen ions.","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 — The respiratory parameter: the partial pressure of CO2 in the artery (the carbonic acid that dissolves into CO2 and water).","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 — The metabolic parameter. It is calculated on the ABG, not directly measured.","Serum CO2 (BMP) — 24 to 29 — On a regular venous chemistry panel (BMP), the CO2 level is equivalent to the bicarb.","The ABG also reports PO2 and O2 saturation, and the slide compares ABG and VBG values (a VBG reports PvCO2, the partial pressure of CO2 in the vein, with a slightly different pH). The transcript doesn't read those numbers aloud, so they are…","Key Terms","Primary event — The problem that initiates the acid-base imbalance: the root cause. — Hypoventilation, hyperventilation, vomiting, diarrhea.","Primary disorder — What happens as a result of the primary event. — Hypoventilation retains CO2 → respiratory acidosis. Vomiting up stomach acid → metabolic alkalosis.","Compensatory mechanisms — The processes the body uses to bring acid-base balance back, without correcting the underlying cause. — A lung problem → the kidneys compensate (and can keep it up longer, if they function properly). A kidney probl…","Metabolic cause — The bicarb level changes because of metabolic alterations (think kidneys). — Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory cause — The carbonic acid (CO2) level changes because of alterations in the lungs. — Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis.","ROME & the Four Primary Disorders","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory: pH and CO2 move in opposite directions (pH up, CO2 down = alkalosis; pH down, CO2 up = acidosis). Metabolic: pH and bicarb move in the same direction (both up = alkalosis; both down …","Metabolic acidosis — Bicarb ↓ — ↓ — CO2 ↓","Metabolic alkalosis — Bicarb ↑ — ↑ — CO2 ↑","Respiratory acidosis — CO2 ↑ — ↓ — Bicarb ↑","Respiratory alkalosis — CO2 ↓ — ↑ — Bicarb ↓","Notice the compensating value always moves in the same direction as the primary change.","Metabolic Alkalosis","Too much bicarb, or not enough carbonic acid (acid).","ABG — pH high; bicarb high (greater than 29); CO2 typically normal if uncompensated, and high once the lungs compensate.","Causes — Excessive baking soda or antacids (Alka-Seltzer): too much base. The lecture links this to hypokalemia, with hydrogen shifting out of the cell and potassium going in. Prolonged vomiting (losing hydrochloric acid, so hydrogen ions).…","Manifestations — CNS over-excitability, tremors, muscle cramps, confusion up to coma, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and respiratory depression (the body holding on to CO2 to counteract the alkalosis).","Cardiac risk — With a high pH, hydrogen ions come out of the cell to buffer, and potassium goes into the cell, so less potassium circulates: hypokalemia, with its classic cardiac issues.","Compensation — The lungs decrease the rate and depth of breathing to hold on to CO2.","Treatment — Treat the cause, such as the vomiting. Drugs that reduce gastric hydrochloric acid secretion: H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors.","Respiratory Alkalosis","The lecture introduces it as a \"bicarb deficit in our extracellular fluid,\" most often caused by hyperventilation (the primary event), with too much CO2 blown off.","ABG — pH high; CO2 low (blowing off CO2); bicarb relatively normal, because the kidneys are not yet compensating.","Why the kidneys can't help acutely — The kidneys take hours to days. A person hyperventilating in a panic attack often passes out; their breathing then regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Causes — Hyperventilation (anxiety, panic attack); increased metabolic demand (fever, infection, sepsis); some medications that increase breathing rate; head injuries, brain lesions or chronic brain injury; pulmonary diseases; ventilator se…","Manifestations — Similar to metabolic alkalosis: CNS over-excitability, light-headedness, syncopal episodes, up to coma.","Treatment — Help retain CO2 and decrease respirations: anti-anxiety medications, calming the patient, breathing into a paper bag (inhaling CO2). On a ventilator, change the settings.","Respiratory Acidosis","Can be acute or chronic. Excess CO2 means too much carbonic acid, so too high a hydrogen ion concentration. Primarily caused by hypoventilation: CO2 rises and pH drops.","Acute (e.g., respiratory arrest)","pH low.","CO2 high.","Bicarb pretty normal: the kidneys haven't had time to compensate.","Chronic (e.g., COPD retaining CO2)","The kidneys have had time to compensate.","Bicarb high.","pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","The COPD Example: Acute on Chronic","This client's baseline (chronic) — 60 — 32 — Normal (chronic, compensated)","Now with an infection (breathing worse) — 75 — Still 32: the kidneys haven't had a chance to compensate — Drops: more acidic","Causes — New-onset CNS depression and hypoventilation: overdose, found down, too much narcotic or sedative given or taken; respiratory arrest; head injury; anesthesia and post-anesthesia (the body can take longer to metabolize the drugs); p…","Manifestations — Difficulty breathing or respiratory distress; slow, shallow breathing; dysrhythmias and ECG changes; decreased level of consciousness, eventually coma. The patient feels like they can't catch their breath.","Interventions — Improve ventilation (increase the rate and depth of breathing to blow off CO2): stimulate the patient, encourage slow and deep breathing; on a ventilator, increase the respiratory rate or the tidal volume.","Metabolic Acidosis","Too much acid or not enough bicarb: a bicarb deficit in the blood.","ABG — pH low (below 7.35); bicarb low (same direction as pH). CO2 somewhat normal early, before the lungs compensate; once they do, the lungs breathe faster to blow off CO2, lowering it.","Causes — Renal disease or failure (can't excrete hydrogen ions; dialysis patients take daily bicarb); fistulas (bicarb lost with fluid and electrolytes); diabetes, especially type 1, with DKA; lactic acidosis (cells without enough oxygen); …","Manifestations — Lethargy, confusion, headache; tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching; paresthesias (tingling of fingers, toes, perioral); hypotension; hyperkalemia (hydrogen ions pulled into the cell, potassium out); warm, flushed skin;…","Compensation — Compensatory hyperventilation: deep breaths called Kussmaul respirations, common in DKA, as the lungs try to blow off CO2 and reduce the acidosis.","Treatment (DKA) — DKA's high ketones are acids. Treat by bringing the blood glucose down with insulin to decrease the acid in the body (more later in the course).","One line in the recording says that if the lungs are compensating, the CO2 \"is going to be increased.\" The rest of the lecture (the arrow table, Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2, and the worked example of 7.35 / 33 / 20) shows the CO2 goi…"]},{"s":"Part 2B — ABG Interpretation▾","a":"part-2b-abg-interpretation","k":["Part 2B — ABG Interpretation▾","Topic 2, second voiceover (ABG Interpretation): how compensation works, the four levels of compensation, the step-by-step method, every worked example, and the two shortcut methods.","How Compensation Works","Respiratory compensation (lungs)","Triggered by a metabolic problem.","Rapid: within a few minutes to a few hours.","Hyperventilation (blow off CO2) or hypoventilation (conserve CO2), depending on whether it's correcting an acidosis or an alkalosis.","Metabolic compensation (kidneys)","Triggered by a respiratory problem.","Slow: hours to days.","Retains or excretes hydrogen ions and bicarb as needed.","The Four Levels of Compensation","Uncompensated — Abnormal — Only one (the acid or the base component) is abnormal, not both.","Partially compensated — Still abnormal — Both are abnormal: the body has started to respond, moving the second component to pull the pH back.","Compensated — Within normal limits — Still abnormal, but balanced. The imbalance is neutralized, which is not the same as corrected.","Corrected — Within normal limits — Also within normal limits, after whatever caused the imbalance has been corrected.","Not everyone returns to \"corrected.\" A COPD patient holds on to CO2, and their bicarb stays high to balance it; because COPD is chronic, you will never see a fully corrected ABG.","Interpreting an ABG Step by Step","1. pH — Acidic, basic or normal? If the pH is normal but the CO2 and bicarb are not, compensation has occurred. Then decide which way the normal pH leans: treat 7.40 as neutral; 7.35 to 7.40 leans acidic, above 7.40 to 7.45 leans basic (e.g…","2. PaCO2 (35 to 45) — Acidic (high), basic (low) or normal? If it is abnormal and matches the pH, it's a respiratory problem: an inverse relationship with pH (Respiratory Opposite).","3. HCO3 (24 to 29) — A calculated value. Basic (high), acidic (low) or normal? If it matches the pH, it's a metabolic problem: a direct relationship with pH (Metabolic Equal).","4. Compensation — Is the body trying to restore the pH by altering the system that is not involved in the imbalance? If compensation has occurred, that value moves in the same direction as the other component. Use the four levels above.","With compensation, the pH points the way. When the pH is normal, the side it leans toward (acidic or basic) tells you which value is the primary problem and which is compensating.","Worked Examples From the Lecture","Cover the interpretation column with the eye button and work each one yourself. Values are pH / PaCO2 / HCO3.","7.25 / 56 / 24 — Respiratory acidosis (no compensation) — pH down, CO2 up (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.35 / 56 / 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the high CO2; the high bicarb is compensating.","7.60 / 25 / 24 — Respiratory alkalosis (no compensation) — pH up, CO2 down (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.45 / 30 / 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis — pH normal but leaning basic, matching the low CO2; the low bicarb is compensating.","7.26 / 40 / 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — pH down, bicarb down (equal); CO2 normal.","7.35 / 33 / 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the low bicarb; the low CO2 is compensating.","7.46 / 37 / 26 — Metabolic alkalosis (as given in the lecture) — pH basic, CO2 normal; the lecture calls the bicarb of 26 \"on the higher end.\"","Normal pH leaning basic / high CO2 / 30 — Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Leaning basic matches the high bicarb; the high CO2 is compensating.","7.30 / 52 / 30 — Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated — pH and CO2 both acidic; the kidneys are retaining bicarb, but the pH is still abnormal.","7.60 / 31 / 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Tic-tac-toe: pH and CO2 line up in the basic column; bicarb is normal.","Below 7.35 / 29 / 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — pH and bicarb both acidic; the CO2 is abnormal (basic), but the pH is not yet normal.","7.52 / 48 / 39 — Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated — pH and bicarb both basic; CO2 abnormal (acidic); pH still abnormal.","The lecture ends with a slide of extra practice ABGs to work through on your own; their values aren't read aloud in the transcript.","Two Shortcut Methods","\"It's important to know this information and to rationalize all these steps.\" These are extra ways to get there, if they fit how you think.","ABG tic-tac-toe","Draw a board with three columns: Acidic, Normal, Basic.","Place the pH, CO2 and bicarb in the column each one falls in.","The value that lines up with the pH names the problem (CO2 = respiratory, bicarb = metabolic), and the pH's column names acidosis or alkalosis.","If the third value is in the normal column, it's uncompensated.","The instructor's three lines","Draw a line each for pH, CO2 and bicarb, always in that order.","pH: low end Acidic → high end Basic.","CO2: opposite, low is Basic → high is Acidic.","Bicarb: low is Acidic → high is Basic.","Memorize \"AB, BA, AB,\" mark where each value falls, and see what matches the pH."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1A. pH, Hydrogen & Acids — More hydrogen ions = lower pH; the scale is logarithmic (each number is 10 times). Normal serum pH 7.35–7.45; below 7.35 is acidosis, above 7.45 alkalosis; below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life. Acidic …","1B. How the Body Defends Its pH — Buffers act in seconds, the lungs in minutes to hours, the kidneys in hours to days. Bicarbonate–carbonic acid is the main extracellular buffer; phosphate and protein buffers are intracellular. Know CO2 + H…","2A. ABGs & the Four Disorders — Know the numbers: pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45, HCO3 24–29 (BMP CO2 = bicarb). ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Metabolic alkalosis: vomiting, NG suction, loop diuretics, antacids (treat with H2 bloc…","2B. ABG Interpretation — Step 1 pH (7.40 is the midpoint), step 2 PaCO2, step 3 HCO3, step 4 compensation. The value that matches the pH is the problem; the other one moving the same direction is compensating. Uncompensated: one abnormal va…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the four parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week2-acid-base-balance.html","t":"Acid-Base Balance","secs":[{"s":"Acid-Base Balance","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 1","What pH Is","Why Hydrogen Matters","Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body","What pH Affects","Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits","Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids","The Three Lines of Defense","Buffer Systems","The Carbonic Acid Equation","Cellular Compensation & Potassium","Respiratory Control of pH","Renal Control of pH","Compensation","What an ABG Measures","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the two acid-base voiceovers. Part 1 covers what pH is, why hydrogen matters, the normal serum range and how pH varies around the body, what pH affects (including oxygen delivery), the survivable limits, and volatile vs n…"]},{"s":"What pH Is▾","a":"what-ph-is","k":["What pH Is▾","pH, acids and bases","pH is the potential, or the power, of a hydrogen ion. It is a scale that identifies whether a solution is more acidic or more alkaline, based on the hydrogen ions present.","The bottom of the scale is more acidic, the middle is neutral, and the higher the number, the more basic (alkaline).","pH is inversely related to hydrogen ion concentration: when hydrogen ions go up, pH goes down (more acidic); when hydrogen ions go down, pH goes up (more basic).","The scale is logarithmic, not linear: each number is 10 times more acidic or alkaline than the one before it. A pH of 5 is 10 times more acidic than a pH of 6; going from 7 to 6 is a tenfold increase in hydrogen ions.","An acid can donate (release) hydrogen ions. A base can accept hydrogen ions; bases have fewer hydrogen ions and give up a hydroxide ion (OH⁻).","A strong acid completely dissociates in solution (gives up all its hydrogen ions). A weak acid only partially dissociates.","Where Common Things Fall on the Scale","Acidic side (lower numbers)","Milk, coffee, tomato, lemon","Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid)","Battery acid","Neutral and alkaline side","Water: 7, neutral","Blood: on the alkaline side; the slide shows it at 8, but it is really a little lower than 8","Baking soda, stomach tablets (like Alka-Seltzer), soap","Drain cleaner: 14"]},{"s":"Why Hydrogen Matters▾","a":"why-hydrogen-matters","k":["Why Hydrogen Matters▾","What hydrogen does in the body","Helps maintain cell membranes.","Helps with enzymatic activity.","Is a component of water and keeps the body hydrated.","Helps with energy production.","Is a component of the sugars, proteins, starches and fats we consume.","The body works to stay in homeostasis and functions best within certain ranges, like a heart rate of 60 to 100 or sodium of 135 to 145. pH is one of those ranges."]},{"s":"Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body▾","a":"normal-ph-how-it-varies-in-the-body","k":["Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body▾","Normal serum (blood) pH: 7.35 to 7.45. Most of the body's enzymes function within that very narrow range.","pH isn't the same everywhere. The stomach is the most acidic place in the body, with hydrochloric acid at a pH more like 1. The stomach is uniquely designed for it: mucous cells secrete mucus and bicarbonate at the cell surface to protect t…","Serum (blood) — 7.35 to 7.45","Saliva — 6.5 to 7.5","Stomach — As low as 1.5 on the GI chart (about 1 in the narration), then pH increases as contents move through the GI tract. The GI tract's differences aid digestion.","Skeletal muscle — 6.9 to 7.2","Bone — 7.4","Liver — 7.2","Pleural fluid — 7.6"]},{"s":"What pH Affects▾","a":"what-ph-affects","k":["What pH Affects▾","Acid-base balance affects","Electrolytes: sodium, potassium in particular, and chloride.","Hormones.","Oxygen transport and delivery, which is greatly affected.","Acidic environment","Hemoglobin gives up oxygen more readily at the tissue.","Alkalotic environment","Hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and doesn't give it to the tissue, which can result in inadequate tissue oxygenation.","The slide shows this on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: the relationship between oxygen saturation of the blood and oxygen partial pressure, which shifts as the blood becomes acidotic or alkalotic."]},{"s":"Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits▾","a":"acidosis-alkalosis-survivable-limits","k":["Acidosis, Alkalosis & Survivable Limits▾","Below 7.35 — Acidosis.","7.35 to 7.45 — Normal.","Above 7.45 — Alkalosis.","Below 6.8 or above 7.8 — Incompatible with life, because of pH's profound effects on cellular metabolism, enzyme activity and oxygen delivery.","The body can function outside the normal range for a short time, but because pH affects so much (neurologic, respiratory, GI and cardiovascular function), the goal is to get back to normal as quickly as possible."]},{"s":"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids▾","a":"volatile-vs-non-volatile-acids","k":["Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids▾","Volatile acids","Can be eliminated as carbon dioxide (CO2) gas, by the lungs (pulmonary ventilation).","The only volatile acid in the body is carbonic acid (H2CO3).","Carbonic acid is a weak acid: it does not easily release its hydrogen ion.","In the presence of the enzyme carbonic anhydrase, it readily dissociates into CO2 and water.","Non-volatile acids","Can be eliminated only by the kidneys.","Most are strong acids: they readily release their hydrogen ions.","Examples: lactic, phosphoric, sulfuric, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid (table below).","Lactic acid — A byproduct of anaerobic metabolism (metabolism without oxygen). A little different: it is metabolized by the liver and kidneys, and once oxygen is reestablished to the tissue it can be reconverted and used for energy again.","Phosphoric acid — Made from phosphorus; works with calcium to help with strong bones, and supports kidney function.","Sulfuric acid — Results from protein metabolism.","Acetoacetic acid — Produced by the liver.","Beta-hydroxybutyric acid — Increases with exercise, calorie restriction and fasting. Its measurement is used as a diagnostic for people in DKA (ketoacidosis).","Why the Body Leans Acidic","At baseline, the body produces more acids than bases: from the food we eat and the drinks we consume, from the metabolism of lipids and proteins, and from cellular metabolism, whose waste product is CO2.","We want to avoid alkalosis, especially if a person is ill, because hemoglobin holds on to oxygen and tissue oxygenation suffers. An alkaline pH is also really difficult to correct."]},{"s":"The Three Lines of Defense▾","a":"the-three-lines-of-defense","k":["The Three Lines of Defense▾","1. Chemical buffer systems — Almost instantaneously (seconds) — Primarily intracellular. Chemicals combine with acid or base to resist a change in pH. Makes only small changes; a relatively short, not sustainable fix.","2. Respiratory system — Several minutes to hours — Changes the rate or depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO2. The lungs are the primary controller of the carbonic acid supply.","3. Renal (kidney) control — Several hours to days — The long-term regulator. Excretes hydrogen ions and retains or excretes bicarb. The kidneys are the primary controller of bicarb (the base).","The lungs and kidneys work together. The lungs respond relatively quickly; the kidneys need more time, but their mechanisms are more long-term."]},{"s":"Buffer Systems▾","a":"buffer-systems","k":["Buffer Systems▾","Buffers are chemicals that combine with an acid or base to change the pH and bring it back to normal. They can release or accept a hydrogen ion, and they work almost instantaneously, but they make small, short-term corrections only.","Bicarbonate–carbonic acid — The main buffer outside the cells (extracellular fluid) — CO2 from cell metabolism combines with water to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase breaks down in the lungs and kidneys (see the equation below)…","Phosphate — The main intracellular buffer — Picks up a hydrogen ion inside the cell to balance the pH inside the cell. It can't be measured. You don't need to memorize its equation.","Protein — Primarily intracellular (most proteins are in cells) — Most proteins can buffer. Carboxyl groups act as weak acids and release hydrogen ions; amino groups act as weak bases and accept them. When pH increases, proteins release hydr…","Hemoglobin — A protein buffer in the blood (some call it a fourth buffer system) — As pH increases, hemoglobin loses hydrogen ions, and the reverse when pH decreases. It also binds CO2 to form carbaminohemoglobin, which carries CO2 to the l…"]},{"s":"The Carbonic Acid Equation▾","a":"the-carbonic-acid-equation","k":["The Carbonic Acid Equation▾","CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 (carbonic acid) ⇌ H⁺ + HCO3⁻ (bicarb). The instructor: \"You really want to be familiar and comfortable with the equation and know this backwards and forwards.\"","Lungs — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into CO2 and water, and the lungs exhale the CO2.","Kidneys — Carbonic anhydrase breaks it down into hydrogen ions and bicarb, and the kidneys excrete the hydrogen ions (and can generate bicarb).","The equation goes both directions: carbonic acid can break down into CO2 and water or form from them, and the same on the bicarb and hydrogen side.","A patient retaining CO2, like a COPD patient, pushes the equation in the other direction and increases bicarb.","CO2 also rises with respiratory depression, such as narcotic use or an overdose: the respiratory rate is so low the lungs can't remove the CO2, which changes the pH.","When a patient is acidotic and not compensating through the respiratory system, sodium bicarb is often given as a quick fix to raise the pH. This happens a lot in the ICU."]},{"s":"Cellular Compensation & Potassium▾","a":"cellular-compensation-potassium","k":["Cellular Compensation & Potassium▾","Cells like a neutral electrical balance. Hydrogen and potassium are both positively charged, and potassium is found primarily inside the cell.","In acidosis — Hydrogen ions move into the cell to balance the pH, and potassium moves out of the cell, which can raise blood potassium.","With kidney dysfunction — The kidneys can't get rid of the excess potassium: hyperkalemia, which can cause dysrhythmias.","When pH returns to normal — Hydrogen ions move back into the blood and potassium moves back into the cells.","With working kidneys — The kidneys will already have excreted the excess potassium, so this can lead to hypokalemia (to be revisited with DKA later in the course).","Cellular compensation is a temporary correction of the pH, not a permanent solution."]},{"s":"Respiratory Control of pH▾","a":"respiratory-control-of-ph","k":["Respiratory Control of pH▾","The body produces CO2 continuously, which forms carbonic acid; in the lungs it breaks back down into water and CO2, and the excess CO2 is exhaled.","This works only on volatile acids, not on non-volatile acids like lactic acid.","The lungs adjust serum pH by changing the rate or depth of breathing. The brain and arterial receptors are stimulated by the pH change.","On mechanical ventilation in the ICU, these adjustments can be made through the ventilator settings.","pH too low (acidosis)","Breathing rate or depth increases → more CO2 blown off → less carbonic acid → pH rises.","pH too high (alkalosis)","Breathing rate or depth decreases → CO2 retained → more carbonic acid → pH falls."]},{"s":"Renal Control of pH▾","a":"renal-control-of-ph","k":["Renal Control of pH▾","The kidneys are the last to alter pH and are the long-term regulators. They can eliminate large amounts of acid by getting rid of hydrogen ions, using an active transport mechanism.","They can also excrete bicarb (a base) if the pH is too high, or retain bicarb if the pH is too low.","Example: in a COPD patient retaining CO2, over time the kidneys compensate by holding on to more bicarb, so this patient has higher CO2 and higher bicarb to balance the serum pH.","Kidney function is extremely important: in renal failure, patients can no longer maintain a normal pH and often take bicarb daily.","Acidosis — Increased — Increased — Raises pH back to normal","Alkalosis — Decreased — Decreased — Lowers pH back to normal","The kidneys handle acidosis better than alkalosis. It is harder for the kidneys to excrete bicarb than to hold on to it."]},{"s":"Compensation▾","a":"compensation","k":["Compensation▾","If one side of the equation is the problem, the body pulls toward the other side to rebalance. That movement is compensation: the body's response to an acid-base imbalance, aimed at bringing the pH back to normal. It is evaluated with arter…","Underlying problem is metabolic","The respiratory system compensates.","Underlying problem is respiratory","The renal system compensates, bringing about metabolic changes (metabolic compensation).","Uncompensated — Nothing has changed in response to a pH outside normal limits.","Partial compensation — Bicarb and CO2 levels have changed, but the pH is still outside normal limits.","Complete compensation — Bicarb or CO2 levels are abnormal, but the pH is back within normal limits.","The four levels of compensation (adding \"corrected\") are covered in ABGs & ABG Interpretation"]},{"s":"What an ABG Measures▾","a":"what-an-abg-measures","k":["What an ABG Measures▾","The lecture ends by previewing the ABG: pH, CO2 (PaCO2), PO2, HCO3 (bicarb) and O2 saturation. \"You do need to know these numbers.\" They are taught in the next lecture.","Normal ABG values are covered in ABGs & ABG Interpretation"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-abg-interpretation.html","t":"ABGs & ABG Interpretation","secs":[{"s":"ABGs & ABG Interpretation","a":"","k":["Week 2 · Topic 2","Why We Draw ABGs","Normal ABG Values","Key Terms","ROME & the Four Primary Disorders","Metabolic Alkalosis","Respiratory Alkalosis","Respiratory Acidosis","Metabolic Acidosis","How Compensation Works","The Four Levels of Compensation","Interpreting an ABG Step by Step","Worked Examples From the Lecture","Two Shortcut Methods","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the ABG voiceover and the ABG interpretation voiceover. It covers why we draw ABGs, the normal values, the key terms (primary event, primary disorder, compensation), the four acid-base disorders with their ABG patterns, c…","pH, buffers, the carbonic acid equation, and how the lungs and kidneys compensate are covered in Acid-Base Balance"]},{"s":"Why We Draw ABGs▾","a":"why-we-draw-abgs","k":["Why We Draw ABGs▾","Purpose of an arterial blood gas","To assess acid-base status, determine the adequacy of oxygenation and ventilation, and decide which treatment would be best.","Arterial blood gives the best representation of the pH and PaCO2 in the body.","A venous blood gas (VBG) can be drawn, but the measurements differ: the CO2 is a bit higher (it's venous blood), which changes the pH measurement. If an arterial draw isn't possible, a VBG can gauge where things stand, but for critically il…"]},{"s":"Normal ABG Values▾","a":"normal-abg-values","k":["Normal ABG Values▾","\"You do need to know these numbers.\" pH 7.35–7.45 · PaCO2 35–45 · HCO3 24–29.","pH — 7.35 to 7.45 — The balance of hydrogen ions.","PaCO2 — 35 to 45 — The respiratory parameter: the partial pressure of CO2 in the artery (the carbonic acid that dissolves into CO2 and water).","HCO3 (bicarb) — 24 to 29 — The metabolic parameter. It is calculated on the ABG, not directly measured.","Serum CO2 (BMP) — 24 to 29 — On a regular venous chemistry panel (BMP), the CO2 level is equivalent to the bicarb.","The ABG also reports PO2 and O2 saturation, and the slide compares ABG and VBG values (a VBG reports PvCO2, the partial pressure of CO2 in the vein, with a slightly different pH). The transcript doesn't read those numbers aloud, so they are…"]},{"s":"Key Terms▾","a":"key-terms","k":["Key Terms▾","Primary event — The problem that initiates the acid-base imbalance: the root cause. — Hypoventilation, hyperventilation, vomiting, diarrhea.","Primary disorder — What happens as a result of the primary event. — Hypoventilation retains CO2 → respiratory acidosis. Vomiting up stomach acid → metabolic alkalosis.","Compensatory mechanisms — The processes the body uses to bring acid-base balance back, without correcting the underlying cause. — A lung problem → the kidneys compensate (and can keep it up longer, if they function properly). A kidney probl…","Metabolic cause — The bicarb level changes because of metabolic alterations (think kidneys). — Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory cause — The carbonic acid (CO2) level changes because of alterations in the lungs. — Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis."]},{"s":"ROME & the Four Primary Disorders▾","a":"rome-the-four-primary-disorders","k":["ROME & the Four Primary Disorders▾","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. Respiratory: pH and CO2 move in opposite directions (pH up, CO2 down = alkalosis; pH down, CO2 up = acidosis). Metabolic: pH and bicarb move in the same direction (both up = alkalosis; both down …","Metabolic acidosis — Bicarb ↓ — ↓ — CO2 ↓","Metabolic alkalosis — Bicarb ↑ — ↑ — CO2 ↑","Respiratory acidosis — CO2 ↑ — ↓ — Bicarb ↑","Respiratory alkalosis — CO2 ↓ — ↑ — Bicarb ↓","Notice the compensating value always moves in the same direction as the primary change."]},{"s":"Metabolic Alkalosis▾","a":"metabolic-alkalosis","k":["Metabolic Alkalosis▾","Too much bicarb, or not enough carbonic acid (acid).","ABG — pH high; bicarb high (greater than 29); CO2 typically normal if uncompensated, and high once the lungs compensate.","Causes — Excessive baking soda or antacids (Alka-Seltzer): too much base. The lecture links this to hypokalemia, with hydrogen shifting out of the cell and potassium going in. Prolonged vomiting (losing hydrochloric acid, so hydrogen ions).…","Manifestations — CNS over-excitability, tremors, muscle cramps, confusion up to coma, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and respiratory depression (the body holding on to CO2 to counteract the alkalosis).","Cardiac risk — With a high pH, hydrogen ions come out of the cell to buffer, and potassium goes into the cell, so less potassium circulates: hypokalemia, with its classic cardiac issues.","Compensation — The lungs decrease the rate and depth of breathing to hold on to CO2.","Treatment — Treat the cause, such as the vomiting. Drugs that reduce gastric hydrochloric acid secretion: H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors.","The hydrogen–potassium shift is covered in Acid-Base Balance"]},{"s":"Respiratory Alkalosis▾","a":"respiratory-alkalosis","k":["Respiratory Alkalosis▾","The lecture introduces it as a \"bicarb deficit in our extracellular fluid,\" most often caused by hyperventilation (the primary event), with too much CO2 blown off.","ABG — pH high; CO2 low (blowing off CO2); bicarb relatively normal, because the kidneys are not yet compensating.","Why the kidneys can't help acutely — The kidneys take hours to days. A person hyperventilating in a panic attack often passes out; their breathing then regulates and CO2 builds back up.","Causes — Hyperventilation (anxiety, panic attack); increased metabolic demand (fever, infection, sepsis); some medications that increase breathing rate; head injuries, brain lesions or chronic brain injury; pulmonary diseases; ventilator se…","Manifestations — Similar to metabolic alkalosis: CNS over-excitability, light-headedness, syncopal episodes, up to coma.","Treatment — Help retain CO2 and decrease respirations: anti-anxiety medications, calming the patient, breathing into a paper bag (inhaling CO2). On a ventilator, change the settings."]},{"s":"Respiratory Acidosis▾","a":"respiratory-acidosis","k":["Respiratory Acidosis▾","Can be acute or chronic. Excess CO2 means too much carbonic acid, so too high a hydrogen ion concentration. Primarily caused by hypoventilation: CO2 rises and pH drops.","Acute (e.g., respiratory arrest)","pH low.","CO2 high.","Bicarb pretty normal: the kidneys haven't had time to compensate.","Chronic (e.g., COPD retaining CO2)","The kidneys have had time to compensate.","Bicarb high.","pH closer to normal, or normal when fully compensated.","The COPD Example: Acute on Chronic","This client's baseline (chronic) — 60 — 32 — Normal (chronic, compensated)","Now with an infection (breathing worse) — 75 — Still 32: the kidneys haven't had a chance to compensate — Drops: more acidic","Causes — New-onset CNS depression and hypoventilation: overdose, found down, too much narcotic or sedative given or taken; respiratory arrest; head injury; anesthesia and post-anesthesia (the body can take longer to metabolize the drugs); p…","Manifestations — Difficulty breathing or respiratory distress; slow, shallow breathing; dysrhythmias and ECG changes; decreased level of consciousness, eventually coma. The patient feels like they can't catch their breath.","Interventions — Improve ventilation (increase the rate and depth of breathing to blow off CO2): stimulate the patient, encourage slow and deep breathing; on a ventilator, increase the respiratory rate or the tidal volume."]},{"s":"Metabolic Acidosis▾","a":"metabolic-acidosis","k":["Metabolic Acidosis▾","Too much acid or not enough bicarb: a bicarb deficit in the blood.","ABG — pH low (below 7.35); bicarb low (same direction as pH). CO2 somewhat normal early, before the lungs compensate; once they do, the lungs breathe faster to blow off CO2, lowering it.","Causes — Renal disease or failure (can't excrete hydrogen ions; dialysis patients take daily bicarb); fistulas (bicarb lost with fluid and electrolytes); diabetes, especially type 1, with DKA; lactic acidosis (cells without enough oxygen); …","Manifestations — Lethargy, confusion, headache; tremors, muscle cramps, muscle twitching; paresthesias (tingling of fingers, toes, perioral); hypotension; hyperkalemia (hydrogen ions pulled into the cell, potassium out); warm, flushed skin;…","Compensation — Compensatory hyperventilation: deep breaths called Kussmaul respirations, common in DKA, as the lungs try to blow off CO2 and reduce the acidosis.","Treatment (DKA) — DKA's high ketones are acids. Treat by bringing the blood glucose down with insulin to decrease the acid in the body (more later in the course).","One line in the recording says that if the lungs are compensating, the CO2 \"is going to be increased.\" The rest of the lecture (the arrow table, Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2, and the worked example of 7.35 / 33 / 20) shows the CO2 goi…"]},{"s":"How Compensation Works▾","a":"how-compensation-works","k":["How Compensation Works▾","Respiratory compensation (lungs)","Triggered by a metabolic problem.","Rapid: within a few minutes to a few hours.","Hyperventilation (blow off CO2) or hypoventilation (conserve CO2), depending on whether it's correcting an acidosis or an alkalosis.","Metabolic compensation (kidneys)","Triggered by a respiratory problem.","Slow: hours to days.","Retains or excretes hydrogen ions and bicarb as needed."]},{"s":"The Four Levels of Compensation▾","a":"the-four-levels-of-compensation","k":["The Four Levels of Compensation▾","Uncompensated — Abnormal — Only one (the acid or the base component) is abnormal, not both.","Partially compensated — Still abnormal — Both are abnormal: the body has started to respond, moving the second component to pull the pH back.","Compensated — Within normal limits — Still abnormal, but balanced. The imbalance is neutralized, which is not the same as corrected.","Corrected — Within normal limits — Also within normal limits, after whatever caused the imbalance has been corrected.","Not everyone returns to \"corrected.\" A COPD patient holds on to CO2, and their bicarb stays high to balance it; because COPD is chronic, you will never see a fully corrected ABG."]},{"s":"Interpreting an ABG Step by Step▾","a":"interpreting-an-abg-step-by-step","k":["Interpreting an ABG Step by Step▾","1. pH — Acidic, basic or normal? If the pH is normal but the CO2 and bicarb are not, compensation has occurred. Then decide which way the normal pH leans: treat 7.40 as neutral; 7.35 to 7.40 leans acidic, above 7.40 to 7.45 leans basic (e.g…","2. PaCO2 (35 to 45) — Acidic (high), basic (low) or normal? If it is abnormal and matches the pH, it's a respiratory problem: an inverse relationship with pH (Respiratory Opposite).","3. HCO3 (24 to 29) — A calculated value. Basic (high), acidic (low) or normal? If it matches the pH, it's a metabolic problem: a direct relationship with pH (Metabolic Equal).","4. Compensation — Is the body trying to restore the pH by altering the system that is not involved in the imbalance? If compensation has occurred, that value moves in the same direction as the other component. Use the four levels above.","With compensation, the pH points the way. When the pH is normal, the side it leans toward (acidic or basic) tells you which value is the primary problem and which is compensating."]},{"s":"Worked Examples From the Lecture▾","a":"worked-examples-from-the-lecture","k":["Worked Examples From the Lecture▾","Cover the interpretation column with the eye button and work each one yourself. Values are pH / PaCO2 / HCO3.","7.25 / 56 / 24 — Respiratory acidosis (no compensation) — pH down, CO2 up (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.35 / 56 / 35 — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the high CO2; the high bicarb is compensating.","7.60 / 25 / 24 — Respiratory alkalosis (no compensation) — pH up, CO2 down (opposite); bicarb normal.","7.45 / 30 / 20 — Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis — pH normal but leaning basic, matching the low CO2; the low bicarb is compensating.","7.26 / 40 / 18 — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — pH down, bicarb down (equal); CO2 normal.","7.35 / 33 / 20 — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — pH normal but leaning acidic, matching the low bicarb; the low CO2 is compensating.","7.46 / 37 / 26 — Metabolic alkalosis (as given in the lecture) — pH basic, CO2 normal; the lecture calls the bicarb of 26 \"on the higher end.\"","Normal pH leaning basic / high CO2 / 30 — Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Leaning basic matches the high bicarb; the high CO2 is compensating.","7.30 / 52 / 30 — Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated — pH and CO2 both acidic; the kidneys are retaining bicarb, but the pH is still abnormal.","7.60 / 31 / 26 — Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Tic-tac-toe: pH and CO2 line up in the basic column; bicarb is normal.","Below 7.35 / 29 / 21 — Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — pH and bicarb both acidic; the CO2 is abnormal (basic), but the pH is not yet normal.","7.52 / 48 / 39 — Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated — pH and bicarb both basic; CO2 abnormal (acidic); pH still abnormal.","The lecture ends with a slide of extra practice ABGs to work through on your own; their values aren't read aloud in the transcript."]},{"s":"Two Shortcut Methods▾","a":"two-shortcut-methods","k":["Two Shortcut Methods▾","\"It's important to know this information and to rationalize all these steps.\" These are extra ways to get there, if they fit how you think.","ABG tic-tac-toe","Draw a board with three columns: Acidic, Normal, Basic.","Place the pH, CO2 and bicarb in the column each one falls in.","The value that lines up with the pH names the problem (CO2 = respiratory, bicarb = metabolic), and the pH's column names acidosis or alkalosis.","If the third value is in the normal column, it's uncompensated.","The instructor's three lines","Draw a line each for pH, CO2 and bicarb, always in that order.","pH: low end Acidic → high end Basic.","CO2: opposite, low is Basic → high is Acidic.","Bicarb: low is Acidic → high is Basic.","Memorize \"AB, BA, AB,\" mark where each value falls, and see what matches the pH."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 2","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics","The Two Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 2, so you can see how the two topics fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week toge…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-two-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Two Topics▾","Each topic page holds two voiceovers, and each voiceover builds directly on the one before it.","The Scale and the Acids — Topic 1, Acid-Base Balance (the sections from \"What pH Is\" through \"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids\") — pH is a measure of hydrogen ions, inversely and logarithmically. The serum has to stay between 7.35 and 7.45, b…","The Defenses — Topic 1, Acid-Base Balance (from \"The Three Lines of Defense\" to the end) — Three lines of defense on three timescales: buffers in seconds, lungs in minutes to hours, kidneys in hours to days. The carbonic acid equation ties …","Reading the Gas — Topic 2, ABGs & ABG Interpretation (from \"Why We Draw ABGs\" through \"Metabolic Acidosis\") — The ABG measures the equation's two ends: PaCO2 is the respiratory side and HCO3 is the metabolic side. That gives four disorders,…","Putting It Together — Topic 2, ABGs & ABG Interpretation (from \"How Compensation Works\" to the end) — A four-step method (pH, PaCO2, HCO3, compensation), four levels of compensation, worked examples, and two shortcuts. Everything from the f…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. The interpretation steps only make sense once ROME and the carbonic acid equation do."]},{"s":"The Two Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-two-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Two Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Acid-Base Balance — More hydrogen ions mean a lower pH, and each step on the scale is tenfold. Serum pH must stay at 7.35–7.45 (below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life); acidic blood releases oxygen to tissues, alkalotic blood …","2 — ABGs & ABG Interpretation — Know pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45 and HCO3 24–29. ROME sorts the four disorders: respiratory acidosis (hypoventilation, overdose, COPD), respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation, panic, high vent settings), metab…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","The carbonic acid equation — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" (know it backwards and forwards) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: it is why PaCO2 is the respiratory value and HCO3 the metabolic value, and why ROME works.","Compensation — Acid-Base Balance: \"Compensation\" (uncompensated, partial, complete; metabolic problems are compensated by the lungs, respiratory problems by the kidneys) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"How Compensation Works\" and \"The Four Le…","Speed of each defense — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Three Lines of Defense\" (seconds, minutes to hours, hours to days) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: respiratory alkalosis shows a normal bicarb because the kidneys can't respond acutely; acute res…","The COPD patient — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" and \"Renal Control of pH\" (retained CO2 pushes bicarb up; the kidneys hold on to bicarb) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: chronic respiratory acidosis, the acute-on-chronic exam…","Potassium shifts — Acid-Base Balance: \"Cellular Compensation & Potassium\" (in acidosis, hydrogen moves in and potassium moves out) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: hyperkalemia in metabolic acidosis; hypokalemia and cardiac issues in metabolic …","DKA and ketones — Acid-Base Balance: beta-hydroxybutyric acid as the DKA diagnostic, and the potassium shift to be revisited with DKA — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"Metabolic Acidosis\" (DKA as a cause, Kussmaul respirations, insulin to lower…","Kidney failure and daily bicarb — Acid-Base Balance: \"Renal Control of pH\" (renal failure can't maintain pH; patients take bicarb daily) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: renal failure as a cause of metabolic acidosis, and dialysis or bicarb whe…","Narcotics and respiratory depression — Acid-Base Balance: \"The Carbonic Acid Equation\" (a low respiratory rate means CO2 can't be removed) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: \"Respiratory Acidosis\" (overdose, too much narcotic or sedative, anesthe…","The ventilator — Acid-Base Balance: \"Respiratory Control of pH\" (ventilator settings can be adjusted to affect pH) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: a rate or tidal volume set too high causes respiratory alkalosis; raising them treats respirator…","Stomach acid and gut bicarb — Acid-Base Balance: \"Normal pH & How It Varies in the Body\" (hydrochloric acid in the stomach) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: vomiting and NG suction remove that acid (metabolic alkalosis, treated with H2 blockers…","Lactic acid — Acid-Base Balance: \"Volatile vs Non-Volatile Acids\" (anaerobic byproduct; the lungs can't remove it) — ABGs & ABG Interpretation: lactic acidosis, shock and cardiac arrest as causes of metabolic acidosis.","Hemoglobin — Acid-Base Balance: \"What pH Affects\" (oxygen release shifts with pH) — The same page's \"Buffer Systems\": hemoglobin as a protein buffer that carries CO2 as carbaminohemoglobin."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and emphasized statements from both pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Acid-Base Balance — A pH below 6.8 or above 7.8 is incompatible with life; outside 7.35–7.45, get back to normal as quickly as possible. Avoid alkalosis in an ill patient (hemoglobin holds on to oxygen). Acidosis can cause hyperkalemia and …","ABGs & ABG Interpretation — Use an ABG, not a VBG, when treatment changes depend on the result in a critically ill patient. Metabolic alkalosis can cause hypokalemia and cardiac issues. Ventilator settings can cause respiratory alkalosis. R…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"Fast fixes are short; slow fixes last.\" Buffers act in seconds but only make small, temporary changes; cellular compensation is temporary; sodium bicarb in the ICU is a quick fix. The lungs act in minutes, but can't compensate for long. Th…","\"The organ that isn't the problem does the compensating.\" A lung problem is compensated by the kidneys (bicarb moves); a metabolic problem is compensated by the lungs (CO2 moves). That's why, in every disorder, the compensating value moves …","\"Breathing is both the cause and the cure.\" Hypoventilation causes respiratory acidosis and is how the lungs compensate for metabolic alkalosis; hyperventilation causes respiratory alkalosis and is how the lungs compensate for metabolic aci…","\"Potassium follows hydrogen in the opposite direction.\" Acidosis drives hydrogen into cells and potassium out (hyperkalemia); alkalosis pulls hydrogen out and potassium in (hypokalemia). Both can affect the heart.","\"Chronic looks calmer than acute.\" A chronic problem gives the kidneys time, so the pH drifts back toward normal (the compensated COPD patient). An acute problem doesn't, so the pH swings (the respiratory arrest, the panic attack, the COPD …"]}]},{"f":"week3-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 3","Part 1 — Migraines","Part 2A — Parkinson's Disease","Part 2B — Parkinson's Pharmacology","Part 3 — Myasthenia Gravis","Part 4 — Multiple Sclerosis","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 3: the four topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: migraines (Part 1). Topic 2: Parkinson's disease and Parkinson's pharmacology (Parts 2A–2B)…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Migraines▾","a":"part-1-migraines","k":["Part 1 — Migraines▾","Topic 1: the definition and diagnostic criteria, types, risk factors and triggers, pathophysiology, the four phases, abortive therapy (analgesics, triptans, CGRP antagonists, antiemetics) and preventive therapy.","What a Migraine Is","Migraines are the most common complaint neurologists see day to day. A migraine is an episodic neurologic disorder characterized by a headache that lasts anywhere from 4 to 72 hours.","At least TWO of these","Unilateral head pain (characteristic of migraine)","Throbbing pain","Headache that worsens with activity","Pain rated moderate to severe","Plus at least ONE of these","Nausea and vomiting","Photophobia: sensitivity to (avoidance of) light, such as sunglasses, dark rooms, pulled curtains","Phonophobia: avoidance of sound; everything feels loud and grating","Types of Migraine","With aura — An aura is present (about a third of people with migraines).","Without aura — No aura. The most common kind of migraine.","Chronic — Migraines on about 15 days a month over 3 months. They usually start out episodic, and their frequency increases over time until they reach that threshold.","Risk Factors & Triggers","Risk factors","Family history.","Estrogen and progesterone: females are more susceptible. Migraines can occur more right before and during menstruation, and often decrease during pregnancy and menopause. The theory is that cyclical withdrawal of these hormones over the men…","Other genetic and environmental factors.","Common triggers","Fatigue; sleep disturbances, meaning too little or too much sleep (e.g., 15 hours when that isn't normal)","Missing a meal; overexertion","Weather changes (rainy, cold weather)","Stress; hormonal changes","Alcohol (red wine is a common one)","Bright lights or strong smells (stores with strong odors)","Pathophysiology","Not well understood; there is no well-established, identifiable pathology. Migraines are associated with changes in brain metabolism and blood flow, and current theories involve changes in neurotransmitter levels and blood vessel tone.","1 — Increased neuronal depolarization results in hyperactivity.","2 — Excitatory neurotransmitters are released in the brain.","3 — They activate the vascular system, stimulating vasodilation, which results in the migraine.","The Four Phases of a Migraine","1. Premonitory — Hours or days before the headache; about a third of people with migraines — Fatigue, irritability, loss of concentration, stiff neck, food cravings.","2. Aura — Only in people with aura (about a third); lasts up to about an hour — Visual: shimmering lights, flashing spots, blind spots. Sensory: numbness, tingling, pins and needles. Motor: weakness, difficulty with speech, coordination pro…","3. Headache — 4 to 72 hours — Throbbing pain, typically starting on one side and spreading to the whole head; fatigue, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, hypersensitivity to touch on the head.","4. Recovery — A few hours up to a few days to fully resolve — Irritability, fatigue, depression.","Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy","Abortive therapy treats the symptoms, and it is more effective when given early in the course of the headache.","Mild to moderate, no severe nausea or vomiting — Basic analgesics: aspirin, acetaminophen, NSAIDs, or a combination. Tried first because they can be really effective, are readily available and are inexpensive. If they don't work alone, a tr…","Moderate to severe, no nausea or vomiting — An oral migraine-specific agent: the triptans, often combined (e.g., sumatriptan with naproxen), since the combination is more effective.","Can't take or tolerate triptans — A CGRP antagonist (calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonist).","Severe nausea and vomiting (any severity of migraine) — Antiemetics are added, such as ondansetron or promethazine (Phenergan).","Triptans (Serotonin 1B/1D Receptor Agonists)","How they work — Promote vasoconstriction of the intracranial blood vessels (vasodilation has a role in causing migraine). They also suppress inflammatory neuropeptides in the brain and block the pain pathway.","Routes — Subcutaneous, PO or intranasal. Nasal spray has fewer side effects than subQ. SubQ works most quickly. PO has side effects similar to subQ, but they occur less often.","Side effects — Injection site reactions (subQ), chest pressure, flushing, weakness, headache; a bad taste with the nasal form. These make sense for a drug that constricts blood vessels.","Avoid in — Ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina.","No triptans for clients with ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina. Triptans work by constricting blood vessels.","CGRP Antagonist: Rimegepant (Nurtec)","Used when triptans aren't effective or are contraindicated: CAD, heart disease, angina, ischemic stroke.","Given PO.","Side effects: GI upset (abdominal pain, nausea, dyspepsia).","A CYP substrate: use caution with any inhibitors or inducers of that system.","Preventive Therapy","When it's used","More than 4 migraines in a month, or","Migraines that last longer than 12 hours.","Drugs named","Beta blockers: propranolol","Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline","Anti-epileptic drugs","Estrogen","Goals of preventive therapy","Reduce migraine frequency, severity and duration.","Improve the response to treatment of acute attacks.","Improve function and reduce disability.","Prevent episodic migraines from turning into chronic migraines."]},{"s":"Part 2A — Parkinson's Disease▾","a":"part-2a-parkinson-s-disease","k":["Part 2A — Parkinson's Disease▾","Topic 2, first voiceover: what PD is, primary vs secondary parkinsonism, risk factors, the dopamine–acetylcholine imbalance, the clinical manifestations and the classic triad, and complications.","What Parkinson's Disease Is","Parkinson's disease (PD)","A complex motor disorder with other systemic non-motor and neurologic symptoms.","Progressive and degenerative: it keeps getting worse, and some symptoms worsen faster than others.","Characterized by tremors, rigidity and bradykinesia. The tremor is notably more present when the muscles are at rest.","A disorder of the basal ganglia, which help produce smooth, coordinated movements (the lecture places them in the cerebellum). The concern is the substantia nigra inside the basal ganglia, which contains the cells that produce dopamine.","Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism","Parkinsonism is the umbrella term. Parkinson's disease is the leading cause of parkinsonism.","Primary parkinsonism","The etiology is unknown (idiopathic); this is Parkinson's disease.","Also includes genetic components and sporadic onset.","Defining feature: no defining event led to the onset of symptoms.","There is damage to the dopamine-producing cells.","Secondary (acquired) parkinsonism","Results from something else: an event that caused the onset.","Infection, head trauma, drug intoxication.","Some antiemetics and some antidepressants are the most common cause. Symptoms usually go away after the medication is stopped.","Any drug that interferes with dopamine transmission can cause it.","Who Gets Parkinson's Disease","Age — Often begins after about 50; incidence rapidly increases after about 60 and peaks around 70. Typically diagnosed in the elderly, but it can happen young (Michael J. Fox, diagnosed in his early 30s).","Genetics — Familial or genetic forms account for about 10% of primary parkinsonism, with both dominant and recessive patterns.","Sex — Seen more often in males.","Environment — Pesticides, air pollution, farm and agricultural work.","Depression — Can make PD develop earlier, because of its association with dopamine changes.","Head trauma — Makes PD more likely (Muhammad Ali: repeated head trauma from boxing, diagnosed in his late 30s).","Decreased estrogen — A hysterectomy, or anything that decreases estrogen, is a risk factor.","Coffee — A protective factor.","Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance","Dopamine","Has both inhibitory and excitatory effects in the brain, depending on the receptor it binds.","In PD, it's the inhibitory effects that are lacking.","Helps control movement and balance by inhibiting unnecessary movements, which improves coordination, balance and muscle control.","Acetylcholine (ACh)","An excitatory neurotransmitter: stimulates muscle movement and can cause uncoordinated movement.","Essential for all muscle function, and works with dopamine. It's all about the balance.","1 — The substantia nigra in the basal ganglia is destroyed.","2 — Dopamine levels decrease, because the dopamine-producing cells are in the substantia nigra.","3 — An imbalance develops: a relative excess of acetylcholine.","4 — Extra muscle movements that dopamine would normally inhibit are no longer inhibited: loss of controlled movement and balance.","Clinical Manifestations","They may develop alone or in combination, and diagnosis often takes a while because onset is insidious. At first it may involve only one side of the body; it typically becomes bilateral as the disease progresses. Later, all of them appear.","Common signs","Bradykinesia: slowness of movement.","Cogwheel rigidity: stiff muscles; movements like bending the elbow aren't smooth.","Resting tremor.","Shuffling (Parkinsonian) gait: they don't lift their feet.","Mask-like expression: loss of small facial movements, a flat affect.","Postural instability.","The classic triad, the hallmark of PD: Tremor, Rigidity, Bradykinesia.","Tremor","Often the first sign. It is a rest tremor: most noticeable when the body part is supported against gravity but not doing purposeful activity, and it decreases with movement.","An early sign is the pill-rolling tremor: the thumb looks like it's rolling a small object against the finger. In the dominant hand, handwriting may suffer.","Worse with stress or concentration, such as trying to control the movement.","Cause — Faulty neurological impulses — Dopamine deficiency and the ACh imbalance","When — Occurs with motor function; fine motor movements are really difficult — Occurs at rest; tends to improve with movement","Other signs — No other manifestations of Parkinson's — Comes with the other manifestations of Parkinson's","Rigidity","Resistance to passive movement: when the provider flexes the client's arm at the elbow, there's unintentional resistance.","Cogwheel rigidity: slow, jerky movements like a cogwheel.","Cause: excess acetylcholine stimulates the muscles continuously, so they never get to relax (sustained contraction). Clients report muscle soreness, aches and pains.","Often unilateral at first, becoming bilateral over time.","Bradykinesia","Loss of automatic movement, or slowness of movement. Arguably the major cause of disability in Parkinson's: a lack of the spontaneous movements we take for granted.","Blinking — Dry eye, more susceptible to abrasion.","Arm swing while walking — Reduced or absent, adding to balance problems.","Swallowing saliva — Drooling.","Facial micro-expressions — Flat affect, mask-like expression.","Postural Instability & Gait","Postural instability: a decreased ability to prevent falling while moving, from impaired postural reflexes.","Gait disturbances: short steps, or shuffling without picking up the feet.","Complications","Dementia — About 40% develop it. Lewy bodies spread from movement areas of the brain into memory and cognition areas. Can also cause hallucinations.","Fatigue — From sleep disturbances: the tremor picks up while lying at rest trying to fall asleep.","Depression — A risk factor and also a complication; they may become dependent on others for the first time.","Anxiety — The second most frequent psychiatric disturbance in PD, after depression.","Decreased mobility — Malnutrition (trouble preparing meals or feeding themselves); aspiration and pneumonia from swallowing problems; UTIs from urinary retention; skin breakdown from sitting more.","Drug-related — The drugs are very effective but come with complications (below)."]},{"s":"Part 2B — Parkinson's Pharmacology▾","a":"part-2b-parkinson-s-pharmacology","k":["Part 2B — Parkinson's Pharmacology▾","Topic 2, second voiceover: the goals of treatment, Sinemet and its loss of effect, adverse effects and interactions, Duopa, the dopamine agonists, rotigotine and apomorphine.","Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy","Goal: help clients keep motor control as long as possible, decrease symptoms, and maintain as much independence as possible. Because PD is progressive and degenerative, treatment is staving it off as much as possible.","When to start: once symptoms affect the dominant hand or interfere with work, ADLs, or social or leisure activities.","Strategy: correct the imbalance, either by enhancing dopamine (dopaminergic drugs) or by blocking acetylcholine (anticholinergic drugs).","Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)","The gold standard of treatment for Parkinson's disease.","Levodopa","Once it reaches the brain, it is converted to dopamine and activates the dopamine receptors, increasing dopamine activity.","Carbidopa","Prevents levodopa from being broken down before it reaches the brain, so more dopamine is sustained for longer.","One of the most effective PD drugs, but it takes several months for symptoms to improve while dopamine builds up. Teach clients it can take up to months.","It does not work long-term: as PD progresses, fewer dopaminergic terminals survive to take up levodopa and convert it to dopamine, so the effect is lost.","Loss of Drug Effect","Abrupt loss (\"on-off\" phenomenon) — Like flipping a switch: the drug is working, then suddenly it isn't and the symptoms are back. Impossible to predict, can happen at any point in the dosing interval, and over time the \"off\" episodes get m…","Gradual loss (\"wearing off\") — Each dose wears off sooner and sooner, even as the dose is increased; symptoms gradually return. Differs between individuals. — Shorter dosing intervals (taking it more often), or changes in the dose amount.","Adverse Effects (Mostly From the Levodopa)","Nausea and vomiting — Take with a small amount of food. Eating does reduce absorption, so it's a balance, but it keeps clients taking the drug. Avoid high-protein food.","Dyskinesias — From annoying up to disabling, sometimes so disabling the client needs a different drug.","Postural (orthostatic) hypotension — Teach clients to change positions slowly.","Dysrhythmias — Monitor cardiac function, especially when the dose has been increased over time.","Psychosis — In 20%: hallucinations, nightmares, even paranoia.","Darkened sweat and urine — Not harmful, but annoying; it can stain clothing.","Malignant melanoma — Sinemet can activate malignant melanoma: do a good skin assessment before starting, to identify suspicious lesions.","Decrease levodopa's effect","Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)","Antipsychotics","Protein","Increase levodopa's effect (to toxicity)","Anticholinergics","MAOIs","Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)","What it is — Another levodopa-carbidopa combination, as a gel infused into the small intestine through a feeding tube or J-tube. Continuous infusion for up to 16 hours a day; it can be stopped while sleeping at night.","Why — Keeps a consistent, continuous blood level. For clients who don't respond to the PO combination, or whose PO dose doesn't last long enough, or who have more or longer \"off\" periods; it helps minimize on-off episodes.","MAOIs — Do not take within 2 weeks of an MAOI for depression: it can cause levodopa toxicity.","Interactions — Antihypertensives, MAOIs, antipsychotics, metoclopramide, isoniazid, iron, vitamins, high protein. Do a good med rec.","Side effects — Falling asleep without warning (like narcolepsy), so not for clients who drive a truck or pilot an aircraft for a living; orthostatic hypotension; hallucinations (visual, auditory, tactile); unusual urges; depression; dyskine…","Tube risks — Placement is a surgery: infection, tube dislodgement, and the other risks of tube placement.","Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs","Pramipexole (Mirapex) — A dopamine receptor agonist: it binds and stimulates dopamine (D2) receptors. Usually monotherapy in younger clients early in PD; combined with levodopa-carbidopa as the client ages or the disease advances (levodopa …","Ropinirole (Requip) — A dopamine receptor agonist for idiopathic PD. Exactly how it works isn't known; animal studies show increased nerve impulses in the substantia nigra. — Similar to other Parkinson's drugs; with long-term use, an increa…","Rotigotine (Neupro) — A once-daily patch, brought back into the U.S. in 2012. — Not described in the lecture.","Apomorphine (Apokyn) — A short-acting subQ injection for fast relief: useful during the \"off\" phase of the on-off phenomenon to regain control of symptoms. — Not described in the lecture."]},{"s":"Part 3 — Myasthenia Gravis▾","a":"part-3-myasthenia-gravis","k":["Part 3 — Myasthenia Gravis▾","Topic 3: what MG is, the neuromuscular junction attack, clinical manifestations, myasthenic crisis, immunosuppressants and cholinesterase inhibitors, cholinergic crisis, and how to tell the two crises apart.","What Myasthenia Gravis Is","Myasthenia gravis (MG)","An acquired autoimmune disease characterized by fluctuating weakness of certain muscle groups.","Commonly affected: ocular muscles (the eyes), bulbar muscles (brainstem region), and the limb and respiratory muscle groups.","The weakness comes from an antibody-mediated attack on the postsynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction: a breakdown in communication between nerve cells and muscle cells.","How It Progresses","Variable from person to person. It often appears first as a mild, insidious case that's easy to push off (\"just a little muscle weakness\").","It can spontaneously go into remission (back to baseline), stabilize (plateau), or cycle through relapses, stable periods and symptom-free periods lasting weeks to months.","It can also progress severely and rapidly.","Because the course is hard to predict, being honest up front with clients about that is key.","Who Gets It","Rare; more common in females and in people aged 10 to 65 (a broad range).","Why the antibodies attack isn't known; there seems to be a genetic component and possible environmental triggers.","Manifestations often first appear during pregnancy or postpartum, or after exposure to certain types of anesthesia.","What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction","1 — Antibodies form against the nicotinic acetylcholine postsynaptic receptors at the neuromuscular junction of skeletal muscle; the receptors are no longer recognized as self.","2 — Anti-acetylcholine antibodies attach to the receptor sites and block them from binding acetylcholine.","3 — Eventually the antibodies destroy the receptor sites.","4 — The body makes enough acetylcholine, but there aren't enough sites for it to bind, so nerve impulse transmission to the muscle is diminished.","Acetylcholine is an excitatory neurotransmitter responsible for muscle contraction (the lecture says smooth muscle). It also has a role in vasodilation, increasing body secretions, and slowing heart rate, which matters for the drugs below.","Clinical Manifestations","Pattern — Insidious onset with fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness; clients can feel weakness starting, rest, and the strength comes back. Symptoms can be vague.","Eye, face, mouth, throat, neck — Often affected first: drooling, difficulty chewing and swallowing, which affects nutrition and raises aspiration risk.","Respiratory muscles — Weak diaphragm and intercostal muscles can impair the ability to breathe or ventilate appropriately.","Shoulders and hips — Not typically affected, but muscle fatigue after exercise can occur there.","The video (Carrie) — Slow speech, slow blinking, a tracheostomy to help clear secretions, a possibly weak cough, difficulty swallowing.","Myasthenic Crisis","An acute exacerbation of muscle weakness","Can develop as MG progresses, typically triggered by a stressor: infection, surgery, emotional distress, pregnancy, a menstrual cycle, or inadequate pharmacotherapy or other drugs (not on enough medication, or not taking it appropriately).","In the second video, a simple cold sent Carrie into crisis: difficulty expanding her lungs, difficulty breathing, needing oxygen.","Myasthenic crisis: watch the respiratory muscles. The big concern is respiratory insufficiency, and these clients are at higher risk for respiratory arrest.","Drug Therapy","Drug therapy is not a cure; the hope is to alter and alleviate symptoms. Several administration routes can be used.","Immunosuppressants","MG is autoimmune, so immunosuppressants, particularly steroids, are used frequently to calm the immune response.","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Cholinesterase is the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine.","Blocking it lets acetylcholine stay in the synaptic junction longer, so it can hopefully find a receptor the antibodies haven't damaged.","They also intensify the effects of the acetylcholine released, hopefully increasing muscle strength.","Neostigmine","A cholinesterase inhibitor (anticholinesterase drug), so essentially a cholinergic drug, used to treat MG symptoms.","Facilitates impulse transmission across the neuromuscular junction, increasing cholinergic action.","At therapeutic levels it affects both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.","Nicotinic receptors","Adrenal and skeletal muscle.","These are the receptors affected by MG.","Muscarinic receptors","Sweat glands and other glands (and the effects on the GI tract, bladder, heart, lungs and eyes below).","Neostigmine stimulates these too, which causes most of its side effects.","Cholinergic vs Anticholinergic Effects","GI tract — Increased motility, diarrhea — Decreased motility, constipation","Secretions — Increased — Decreased (dries secretions)","Bladder — Urinary urgency — The opposite","Heart rate — Slows — Increases","Lungs — Bronchial constriction — The opposite","Pupils — Miosis (constriction) — The opposite","Use the symptoms to pick the drug: symptomatic bradycardia → an anticholinergic like atropine raises the heart rate. Atropine is also used to dry up secretions, for example for clients on comfort care or hospice who are choking on secretion…","SLUDGE: the cholinergic side effects. Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Diaphoresis, GI distress, Emesis.","Adverse Effects of Neostigmine","Muscarinic stimulation","Increased secretions and GI motility (diarrhea).","Urinary urgency.","Slowed heart rate.","Bronchoconstriction: consider carefully before giving to clients with asthma or other lung disease.","Miosis.","Nicotinic (neuromuscular) stimulation","Therapeutic dose: increased muscle contraction, the desired effect.","Toxic dose: decreased contraction; the overstimulated muscles wear out and can fail, leading to a cholinergic crisis.","Cholinergic Crisis","Extreme muscle weakness or paralysis, plus signs of excessive muscarinic stimulation: increased intestinal motility (diarrhea, cramping), bradycardia, pupil constriction, lots of saliva, diaphoresis.","It arises from anticholinesterase medications: essentially a drug toxicity, from excessive acetylcholine accumulation.","Risk of respiratory arrest; the client may need mechanical ventilation.","Antidote for the muscarinic signs: atropine (an anticholinergic).","Teach clients with MG to wear a medical alert bracelet at all times.","Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis","Both present with respiratory insufficiency and extreme muscle weakness, heading the same direction for opposite reasons.","The problem — Not enough stimulation or acetylcholine — Too much acetylcholine (too much neostigmine)","Why the muscles fail — Too weak, leading to respiratory failure — Overstimulated and worn out, leading to respiratory failure","Typical cause — A stressor, or inadequate medication — Anticholinesterase drug toxicity","After edrophonium — Improves (acetylcholine rises temporarily) — Worsens (already too much acetylcholine)","Edrophonium is a very short-acting cholinesterase inhibitor that raises acetylcholine temporarily. Give it when one of the two crises is suspected: improvement means myasthenic crisis; worsening means cholinergic crisis."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Multiple Sclerosis▾","a":"part-4-multiple-sclerosis","k":["Part 4 — Multiple Sclerosis▾","Topic 4: what MS is, risk factors, pathophysiology, the types of MS, clinical manifestations, disease-modifying drugs, acute relapse treatment and symptom management.","What Multiple Sclerosis Is","Multiple sclerosis (MS)","A chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory disease involving degeneration of CNS myelin (loss of the myelin sheath), scarring (sclerosis or plaque formation) and loss of axons.","A progressive disease, with several types.","Features: lots of inflammation, especially in the white matter of the brain and spinal cord; demyelination of nerve fibers; and scar tissue called gliosis. Glial cells myelinate neurons in the CNS; on imaging, the white spots are areas of s…","Demyelinating disorders damage the myelin sheath and impair neural signal transmission. MS affects the central nervous system; Guillain-Barré affects the peripheral nervous system (a later lecture).","Who Gets MS","The etiology is largely unknown, most likely a combination of genetic predisposition, an immune system problem and an environmental trigger (such as an infection).","Age — Onset typically between 20 and 40.","Sex — More common in females, but males who get MS typically have a more severe, progressive course.","Location — Higher prevalence in moderately cool climates, especially the northern U.S.: above the 40th parallel north and below the 40th parallel south. Areas in between have lower prevalence.","Race — People who are white are more likely to have MS.","Family history — Increases risk.","Possible (associated) — Smoking, vitamin D deficiency, obesity, infection. Not concrete risk factors, but there is evidence of an association.","Pathophysiology","MS is a diffuse, progressive CNS autoimmune inflammatory disease affecting white and gray matter throughout the brain and spinal cord. The attack targets the myelin sheath and involves T lymphocytes and antibodies.","1 — T lymphocytes migrate to the CNS, cross the blood-brain barrier, and increase inflammation and breakdown of the myelin sheath.","2 — An antigen-antibody reaction in the CNS attacks the myelin, releasing inflammatory mediators.","3 — The oligodendrocytes (myelin-producing cells) are lost.","4 — Axons are demyelinated, and plaque and scar tissue form (sclerotic lesions).","5 — The axons die: nerve signals are first delayed, then lost. Neurons die, and the brain atrophies overall.","Normal — An axon wrapped in an intact myelin sheath. — Quick, almost instantaneous transmission.","Inflamed (early) — The nerve fiber is inflamed but intact. — Still transmitted, maybe a little slower; the client may notice some weakness.","Detached — The myelin detaches and the underlying axon is damaged; scarring and axon death follow. — Completely blocked: a permanent loss of function.","By diagnosis, clients are usually quite symptomatic. Looking back, many realize they'd had weakness much longer than they thought, but brushed it off until they couldn't ignore it.","Types of MS","Most types have exacerbations (relapses or flares): a temporary worsening of symptoms, mild or serious, lasting several days to several weeks, which may improve and may be followed by progressively worsening symptoms.","Benign MS — Exacerbations, but after each flare the client returns to baseline: no loss of function.","Relapsing-remitting (RRMS) — The most common type, about 80–90% of cases. Long remissions with a few exacerbations; pretty stable between them, with minimal disability or progression. They don't necessarily return fully to baseline: a littl…","Primary progressive (PPMS) — Gradual, steady progression from the start, with no periods of remission or exacerbation (no flare-ups).","Secondary progressive (SPMS) — Starts with relapses and remissions, with some loss of function each time, then shifts to a more progressive course without remissions.","Progressive relapsing (PRMS) — Gradual progression of symptoms with exacerbations, and fewer remissions overall.","The type is determined by how often exacerbations happen and how long remissions last.","Relapses vary: for about 20% of people they last less than a week; for others 1 to 2 weeks, or 2 weeks to a month.","If relapses get shorter after starting treatment (e.g., from a month down to a week or less), that suggests the medication is working.","Clinical Manifestations","No finding is unique to MS, but some are highly characteristic. The big takeaway: symptoms depend on which nerves are affected.","Common initial symptoms","Paresthesias of the face, trunk and limbs; numbness, tingling, weakness, loss of muscle function.","Optic neuritis and unilateral vision loss.","Impaired gait.","Urinary incontinence.","Some develop seizures.","Symptoms to monitor closely","Cognitive \"cog fog\": difficulty thinking clearly and concentrating, not necessarily a memory problem.","Vision problems.","Depression, related to loss of function.","Fatigue: needs more rest periods.","Pain: chronic (continuous) or acute (with exacerbations); burning or stabbing.","Bowel: constipation most common, possibly incontinence. Bladder: incontinence or retention.","Sexual dysfunction; muscle stiffness or spasms.","Walking and balance issues, vertigo: high fall risk; some eventually need a wheelchair.","Clients with MS are at high risk for falls. Most have walking and balance issues, and some have vertigo.","Goals of Drug Therapy","There is no cure. Goals: slow disability and keep the client functioning at the highest possible capacity.","Decrease relapse frequency, lengthen remissions, and reduce new brain lesions (which lead to more dysfunction and symptoms).","Three jobs: modify the disease process, treat acute exacerbations, and manage symptoms.","Disease-Modifying Drugs","Injectables","Interferon beta (Avonex) — Interferons occur naturally in the immune system and can be pro- or anti-inflammatory. Here they keep pro-inflammatory T cells from crossing the blood-brain barrier, reducing inflammation. Decreases relapse rates …","Glatiramer acetate (Copaxone) — Increases anti-inflammatory T cells, which cross the blood-brain barrier and suppress inflammation. Efficacy similar to interferons. — Injection site reactions. A reaction right after injection: flushing, pal…","Oral Drugs","Fingolimod — Retains lymphocytes in the lymph nodes so they don't migrate across the blood-brain barrier, decreasing brain inflammation. Commonly used for RRMS.","Dimethyl fumarate — Developed specifically for MS. Thought to inhibit immune cells, decreasing inflammation and preventing nerve damage.","Infusions","Natalizumab — Keeps circulating T cells from leaving the blood vessels, so they can't cross the blood-brain barrier. Very effective: reduces relapses by up to 68%. Also used for Crohn's disease. Monotherapy only; used for RRMS. — Most commo…","Alemtuzumab — Reserved for poor response to two or more MS drugs: a last effort, not first-line. — Not described in the lecture.","Mitoxantrone — For secondary progressive MS, progressive relapsing MS, and worsening RRMS that never gets a complete remission. — Not described in the lecture.","Natalizumab and PML: only through the TOUCH program, only as monotherapy, and the PML risk is significantly higher with other immunosuppressants like steroids.","Treating an Acute Relapse","High-dose IV glucocorticoids (\"pulse-dose steroids\") — MS is autoimmune, so very high doses calm the immune response: about 500 mg to 1 g daily for 3 to 5 days (vs 40 to 60 mg for asthma). Not for long-term or frequent use; usually reserved…","IV gamma globulin (IVIG) — For clients who can't tolerate high-dose glucocorticoids.","ACTH (H.P. Acthar Gel) — An adrenocorticotropic hormone gel with prolonged ACTH release after injection. Mechanism not clear, but it treats acute relapses. For clients who can't tolerate glucocorticoids, or when steroids aren't (or weren't)…","Plasmapheresis — A dialysis-like process: blood is removed through a catheter, the plasma is separated from the red cells and replaced with donor plasma, and the blood is reinfused.","Managing MS Symptoms","Symptoms vary from client to client, so medications are chosen based on the presentation.","Urinary frequency — An anticholinergic.","Urinary retention — A cholinergic.","Constipation — Hydration and fiber first, then a bulk-forming laxative.","Fatigue — Amantadine, plus teaching to plan the day around rest periods.","Muscle spasms — Muscle relaxants.","Severe cognitive dysfunction (cog fog) — Donepezil (Aricept), normally an Alzheimer's medication."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1. Migraines — 4 to 72 hours; at least two of unilateral, throbbing, worse with activity, moderate to severe, plus at least one of nausea and vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia. Without aura is most common; chronic = 15 days a month for 3 m…","2A. Parkinson's Disease — Substantia nigra loss → low dopamine → relative excess acetylcholine. Triad: rest tremor (pill-rolling, better with movement, worse with stress), rigidity (cogwheel) and bradykinesia (the major cause of disability:…","2B. Parkinson's Pharmacology — Correct the imbalance: boost dopamine or block acetylcholine. Sinemet is the gold standard (levodopa → dopamine; carbidopa protects levodopa); months to work; on-off and wearing-off loss of effect; avoid high …","3. Myasthenia Gravis — Autoimmune attack on nicotinic ACh receptors at the neuromuscular junction (ACh supply is fine). Fluctuating weakness that improves with rest; eyes and face first; chewing and swallowing (aspiration); respiratory musc…","4. Multiple Sclerosis — T cells cross the blood-brain barrier and attack CNS myelin; oligodendrocytes are lost; scarring; axon death means permanent loss. Age 20–40, female (males worse), cool climates, white, family history. RRMS is most c…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the five parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week3-migraines.html","t":"Migraines","secs":[{"s":"Migraines","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 1","What a Migraine Is","Risk Factors & Triggers","Pathophysiology","The Four Phases of a Migraine","Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy","Preventive Therapy","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the migraine voiceover: the definition and diagnostic criteria, the types, risk factors and triggers, the (still poorly understood) pathophysiology, the four phases, and the two kinds of drug therapy: abortive (symptomatic…"]},{"s":"What a Migraine Is▾","a":"what-a-migraine-is","k":["What a Migraine Is▾","Migraines are the most common complaint neurologists see day to day. A migraine is an episodic neurologic disorder characterized by a headache that lasts anywhere from 4 to 72 hours.","At least TWO of these","Unilateral head pain (characteristic of migraine)","Throbbing pain","Headache that worsens with activity","Pain rated moderate to severe","Plus at least ONE of these","Nausea and vomiting","Photophobia: sensitivity to (avoidance of) light, such as sunglasses, dark rooms, pulled curtains","Phonophobia: avoidance of sound; everything feels loud and grating","Types of Migraine","With aura — An aura is present (about a third of people with migraines).","Without aura — No aura. The most common kind of migraine.","Chronic — Migraines on about 15 days a month over 3 months. They usually start out episodic, and their frequency increases over time until they reach that threshold."]},{"s":"Risk Factors & Triggers▾","a":"risk-factors-triggers","k":["Risk Factors & Triggers▾","Risk factors","Family history.","Estrogen and progesterone: females are more susceptible. Migraines can occur more right before and during menstruation, and often decrease during pregnancy and menopause. The theory is that cyclical withdrawal of these hormones over the men…","Other genetic and environmental factors.","Common triggers","Fatigue; sleep disturbances, meaning too little or too much sleep (e.g., 15 hours when that isn't normal)","Missing a meal; overexertion","Weather changes (rainy, cold weather)","Stress; hormonal changes","Alcohol (red wine is a common one)","Bright lights or strong smells (stores with strong odors)"]},{"s":"Pathophysiology▾","a":"pathophysiology","k":["Pathophysiology▾","Not well understood; there is no well-established, identifiable pathology. Migraines are associated with changes in brain metabolism and blood flow, and current theories involve changes in neurotransmitter levels and blood vessel tone.","1 — Increased neuronal depolarization results in hyperactivity.","2 — Excitatory neurotransmitters are released in the brain.","3 — They activate the vascular system, stimulating vasodilation, which results in the migraine."]},{"s":"The Four Phases of a Migraine▾","a":"the-four-phases-of-a-migraine","k":["The Four Phases of a Migraine▾","1. Premonitory — Hours or days before the headache; about a third of people with migraines — Fatigue, irritability, loss of concentration, stiff neck, food cravings.","2. Aura — Only in people with aura (about a third); lasts up to about an hour — Visual: shimmering lights, flashing spots, blind spots. Sensory: numbness, tingling, pins and needles. Motor: weakness, difficulty with speech, coordination pro…","3. Headache — 4 to 72 hours — Throbbing pain, typically starting on one side and spreading to the whole head; fatigue, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, hypersensitivity to touch on the head.","4. Recovery — A few hours up to a few days to fully resolve — Irritability, fatigue, depression."]},{"s":"Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy▾","a":"abortive-symptomatic-therapy","k":["Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy▾","Abortive therapy treats the symptoms, and it is more effective when given early in the course of the headache.","Mild to moderate, no severe nausea or vomiting — Basic analgesics: aspirin, acetaminophen, NSAIDs, or a combination. Tried first because they can be really effective, are readily available and are inexpensive. If they don't work alone, a tr…","Moderate to severe, no nausea or vomiting — An oral migraine-specific agent: the triptans, often combined (e.g., sumatriptan with naproxen), since the combination is more effective.","Can't take or tolerate triptans — A CGRP antagonist (calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonist).","Severe nausea and vomiting (any severity of migraine) — Antiemetics are added, such as ondansetron or promethazine (Phenergan).","Triptans (Serotonin 1B/1D Receptor Agonists)","How they work — Promote vasoconstriction of the intracranial blood vessels (vasodilation has a role in causing migraine). They also suppress inflammatory neuropeptides in the brain and block the pain pathway.","Routes — Subcutaneous, PO or intranasal. Nasal spray has fewer side effects than subQ. SubQ works most quickly. PO has side effects similar to subQ, but they occur less often.","Side effects — Injection site reactions (subQ), chest pressure, flushing, weakness, headache; a bad taste with the nasal form. These make sense for a drug that constricts blood vessels.","Avoid in — Ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina.","No triptans for clients with ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina. Triptans work by constricting blood vessels.","CGRP Antagonist: Rimegepant (Nurtec)","Used when triptans aren't effective or are contraindicated: CAD, heart disease, angina, ischemic stroke.","Given PO.","Side effects: GI upset (abdominal pain, nausea, dyspepsia).","A CYP substrate: use caution with any inhibitors or inducers of that system."]},{"s":"Preventive Therapy▾","a":"preventive-therapy","k":["Preventive Therapy▾","When it's used","More than 4 migraines in a month, or","Migraines that last longer than 12 hours.","Drugs named","Beta blockers: propranolol","Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline","Anti-epileptic drugs","Estrogen","Goals of preventive therapy","Reduce migraine frequency, severity and duration.","Improve the response to treatment of acute attacks.","Improve function and reduce disability.","Prevent episodic migraines from turning into chronic migraines."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-parkinsons.html","t":"Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 2","What Parkinson's Disease Is","Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism","Who Gets Parkinson's Disease","Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance","Clinical Manifestations","Complications","Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy","Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)","Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)","Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines the two Parkinson's voiceovers. Part 1 covers what Parkinson's disease is, primary vs secondary parkinsonism, risk factors, the dopamine–acetylcholine balance, the clinical manifestations (the classic triad, plus postural…"]},{"s":"What Parkinson's Disease Is▾","a":"what-parkinson-s-disease-is","k":["What Parkinson's Disease Is▾","Parkinson's disease (PD)","A complex motor disorder with other systemic non-motor and neurologic symptoms.","Progressive and degenerative: it keeps getting worse, and some symptoms worsen faster than others.","Characterized by tremors, rigidity and bradykinesia. The tremor is notably more present when the muscles are at rest.","A disorder of the basal ganglia, which help produce smooth, coordinated movements (the lecture places them in the cerebellum). The concern is the substantia nigra inside the basal ganglia, which contains the cells that produce dopamine."]},{"s":"Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism▾","a":"primary-vs-secondary-parkinsonism","k":["Primary vs Secondary Parkinsonism▾","Parkinsonism is the umbrella term. Parkinson's disease is the leading cause of parkinsonism.","Primary parkinsonism","The etiology is unknown (idiopathic); this is Parkinson's disease.","Also includes genetic components and sporadic onset.","Defining feature: no defining event led to the onset of symptoms.","There is damage to the dopamine-producing cells.","Secondary (acquired) parkinsonism","Results from something else: an event that caused the onset.","Infection, head trauma, drug intoxication.","Some antiemetics and some antidepressants are the most common cause. Symptoms usually go away after the medication is stopped.","Any drug that interferes with dopamine transmission can cause it."]},{"s":"Who Gets Parkinson's Disease▾","a":"who-gets-parkinson-s-disease","k":["Who Gets Parkinson's Disease▾","Age — Often begins after about 50; incidence rapidly increases after about 60 and peaks around 70. Typically diagnosed in the elderly, but it can happen young (Michael J. Fox, diagnosed in his early 30s).","Genetics — Familial or genetic forms account for about 10% of primary parkinsonism, with both dominant and recessive patterns.","Sex — Seen more often in males.","Environment — Pesticides, air pollution, farm and agricultural work.","Depression — Can make PD develop earlier, because of its association with dopamine changes.","Head trauma — Makes PD more likely (Muhammad Ali: repeated head trauma from boxing, diagnosed in his late 30s).","Decreased estrogen — A hysterectomy, or anything that decreases estrogen, is a risk factor.","Coffee — A protective factor."]},{"s":"Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance▾","a":"dopamine-acetylcholine-the-imbalance","k":["Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance▾","Dopamine","Has both inhibitory and excitatory effects in the brain, depending on the receptor it binds.","In PD, it's the inhibitory effects that are lacking.","Helps control movement and balance by inhibiting unnecessary movements, which improves coordination, balance and muscle control.","Acetylcholine (ACh)","An excitatory neurotransmitter: stimulates muscle movement and can cause uncoordinated movement.","Essential for all muscle function, and works with dopamine. It's all about the balance.","1 — The substantia nigra in the basal ganglia is destroyed.","2 — Dopamine levels decrease, because the dopamine-producing cells are in the substantia nigra.","3 — An imbalance develops: a relative excess of acetylcholine.","4 — Extra muscle movements that dopamine would normally inhibit are no longer inhibited: loss of controlled movement and balance."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"clinical-manifestations","k":["Clinical Manifestations▾","They may develop alone or in combination, and diagnosis often takes a while because onset is insidious. At first it may involve only one side of the body; it typically becomes bilateral as the disease progresses. Later, all of them appear.","Common signs","Bradykinesia: slowness of movement.","Cogwheel rigidity: stiff muscles; movements like bending the elbow aren't smooth.","Resting tremor.","Shuffling (Parkinsonian) gait: they don't lift their feet.","Mask-like expression: loss of small facial movements, a flat affect.","Postural instability.","The classic triad, the hallmark of PD: Tremor, Rigidity, Bradykinesia.","Tremor","Often the first sign. It is a rest tremor: most noticeable when the body part is supported against gravity but not doing purposeful activity, and it decreases with movement.","An early sign is the pill-rolling tremor: the thumb looks like it's rolling a small object against the finger. In the dominant hand, handwriting may suffer.","Worse with stress or concentration, such as trying to control the movement.","Cause — Faulty neurological impulses — Dopamine deficiency and the ACh imbalance","When — Occurs with motor function; fine motor movements are really difficult — Occurs at rest; tends to improve with movement","Other signs — No other manifestations of Parkinson's — Comes with the other manifestations of Parkinson's","Rigidity","Resistance to passive movement: when the provider flexes the client's arm at the elbow, there's unintentional resistance.","Cogwheel rigidity: slow, jerky movements like a cogwheel.","Cause: excess acetylcholine stimulates the muscles continuously, so they never get to relax (sustained contraction). Clients report muscle soreness, aches and pains.","Often unilateral at first, becoming bilateral over time.","Bradykinesia","Loss of automatic movement, or slowness of movement. Arguably the major cause of disability in Parkinson's: a lack of the spontaneous movements we take for granted.","Blinking — Dry eye, more susceptible to abrasion.","Arm swing while walking — Reduced or absent, adding to balance problems.","Swallowing saliva — Drooling.","Facial micro-expressions — Flat affect, mask-like expression.","Postural Instability & Gait","Postural instability: a decreased ability to prevent falling while moving, from impaired postural reflexes.","Gait disturbances: short steps, or shuffling without picking up the feet."]},{"s":"Complications▾","a":"complications","k":["Complications▾","Dementia — About 40% develop it. Lewy bodies spread from movement areas of the brain into memory and cognition areas. Can also cause hallucinations.","Fatigue — From sleep disturbances: the tremor picks up while lying at rest trying to fall asleep.","Depression — A risk factor and also a complication; they may become dependent on others for the first time.","Anxiety — The second most frequent psychiatric disturbance in PD, after depression.","Decreased mobility — Malnutrition (trouble preparing meals or feeding themselves); aspiration and pneumonia from swallowing problems; UTIs from urinary retention; skin breakdown from sitting more.","Drug-related — The drugs are very effective but come with complications (below)."]},{"s":"Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy▾","a":"goals-strategy-of-drug-therapy","k":["Goals & Strategy of Drug Therapy▾","Goal: help clients keep motor control as long as possible, decrease symptoms, and maintain as much independence as possible. Because PD is progressive and degenerative, treatment is staving it off as much as possible.","When to start: once symptoms affect the dominant hand or interfere with work, ADLs, or social or leisure activities.","Strategy: correct the imbalance, either by enhancing dopamine (dopaminergic drugs) or by blocking acetylcholine (anticholinergic drugs)."]},{"s":"Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)▾","a":"sinemet-levodopa-carbidopa","k":["Sinemet (Levodopa-Carbidopa)▾","The gold standard of treatment for Parkinson's disease.","Levodopa","Once it reaches the brain, it is converted to dopamine and activates the dopamine receptors, increasing dopamine activity.","Carbidopa","Prevents levodopa from being broken down before it reaches the brain, so more dopamine is sustained for longer.","One of the most effective PD drugs, but it takes several months for symptoms to improve while dopamine builds up. Teach clients it can take up to months.","It does not work long-term: as PD progresses, fewer dopaminergic terminals survive to take up levodopa and convert it to dopamine, so the effect is lost.","Loss of Drug Effect","Abrupt loss (\"on-off\" phenomenon) — Like flipping a switch: the drug is working, then suddenly it isn't and the symptoms are back. Impossible to predict, can happen at any point in the dosing interval, and over time the \"off\" episodes get m…","Gradual loss (\"wearing off\") — Each dose wears off sooner and sooner, even as the dose is increased; symptoms gradually return. Differs between individuals. — Shorter dosing intervals (taking it more often), or changes in the dose amount.","Adverse Effects (Mostly From the Levodopa)","Nausea and vomiting — Take with a small amount of food. Eating does reduce absorption, so it's a balance, but it keeps clients taking the drug. Avoid high-protein food.","Dyskinesias — From annoying up to disabling, sometimes so disabling the client needs a different drug.","Postural (orthostatic) hypotension — Teach clients to change positions slowly.","Dysrhythmias — Monitor cardiac function, especially when the dose has been increased over time.","Psychosis — In 20%: hallucinations, nightmares, even paranoia.","Darkened sweat and urine — Not harmful, but annoying; it can stain clothing.","Malignant melanoma — Sinemet can activate malignant melanoma: do a good skin assessment before starting, to identify suspicious lesions.","Decrease levodopa's effect","Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)","Antipsychotics","Protein","Increase levodopa's effect (to toxicity)","Anticholinergics","MAOIs"]},{"s":"Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)▾","a":"duopa-levodopa-carbidopa-infusion","k":["Duopa (Levodopa-Carbidopa Infusion)▾","What it is — Another levodopa-carbidopa combination, as a gel infused into the small intestine through a feeding tube or J-tube. Continuous infusion for up to 16 hours a day; it can be stopped while sleeping at night.","Why — Keeps a consistent, continuous blood level. For clients who don't respond to the PO combination, or whose PO dose doesn't last long enough, or who have more or longer \"off\" periods; it helps minimize on-off episodes.","MAOIs — Do not take within 2 weeks of an MAOI for depression: it can cause levodopa toxicity.","Interactions — Antihypertensives, MAOIs, antipsychotics, metoclopramide, isoniazid, iron, vitamins, high protein. Do a good med rec.","Side effects — Falling asleep without warning (like narcolepsy), so not for clients who drive a truck or pilot an aircraft for a living; orthostatic hypotension; hallucinations (visual, auditory, tactile); unusual urges; depression; dyskine…","Tube risks — Placement is a surgery: infection, tube dislodgement, and the other risks of tube placement."]},{"s":"Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs▾","a":"dopamine-agonists-other-drugs","k":["Dopamine Agonists & Other Drugs▾","Pramipexole (Mirapex) — A dopamine receptor agonist: it binds and stimulates dopamine (D2) receptors. Usually monotherapy in younger clients early in PD; combined with levodopa-carbidopa as the client ages or the disease advances (levodopa …","Ropinirole (Requip) — A dopamine receptor agonist for idiopathic PD. Exactly how it works isn't known; animal studies show increased nerve impulses in the substantia nigra. — Similar to other Parkinson's drugs; with long-term use, an increa…","Rotigotine (Neupro) — A once-daily patch, brought back into the U.S. in 2012. — Not described in the lecture.","Apomorphine (Apokyn) — A short-acting subQ injection for fast relief: useful during the \"off\" phase of the on-off phenomenon to regain control of symptoms. — Not described in the lecture."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-myasthenia-gravis.html","t":"Myasthenia Gravis","secs":[{"s":"Myasthenia Gravis","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 3","What Myasthenia Gravis Is","What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction","Clinical Manifestations","Myasthenic Crisis","Drug Therapy","Cholinergic Crisis","Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the myasthenia gravis voiceover: what MG is and how it progresses, who gets it, what happens at the neuromuscular junction, the clinical manifestations, myasthenic crisis, the drugs (immunosuppressants and cholinesterase i…"]},{"s":"What Myasthenia Gravis Is▾","a":"what-myasthenia-gravis-is","k":["What Myasthenia Gravis Is▾","Myasthenia gravis (MG)","An acquired autoimmune disease characterized by fluctuating weakness of certain muscle groups.","Commonly affected: ocular muscles (the eyes), bulbar muscles (brainstem region), and the limb and respiratory muscle groups.","The weakness comes from an antibody-mediated attack on the postsynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction: a breakdown in communication between nerve cells and muscle cells.","How It Progresses","Variable from person to person. It often appears first as a mild, insidious case that's easy to push off (\"just a little muscle weakness\").","It can spontaneously go into remission (back to baseline), stabilize (plateau), or cycle through relapses, stable periods and symptom-free periods lasting weeks to months.","It can also progress severely and rapidly.","Because the course is hard to predict, being honest up front with clients about that is key.","Who Gets It","Rare; more common in females and in people aged 10 to 65 (a broad range).","Why the antibodies attack isn't known; there seems to be a genetic component and possible environmental triggers.","Manifestations often first appear during pregnancy or postpartum, or after exposure to certain types of anesthesia."]},{"s":"What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction▾","a":"what-happens-at-the-neuromuscular-junction","k":["What Happens at the Neuromuscular Junction▾","1 — Antibodies form against the nicotinic acetylcholine postsynaptic receptors at the neuromuscular junction of skeletal muscle; the receptors are no longer recognized as self.","2 — Anti-acetylcholine antibodies attach to the receptor sites and block them from binding acetylcholine.","3 — Eventually the antibodies destroy the receptor sites.","4 — The body makes enough acetylcholine, but there aren't enough sites for it to bind, so nerve impulse transmission to the muscle is diminished.","Acetylcholine is an excitatory neurotransmitter responsible for muscle contraction (the lecture says smooth muscle). It also has a role in vasodilation, increasing body secretions, and slowing heart rate, which matters for the drugs below."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"clinical-manifestations","k":["Clinical Manifestations▾","Pattern — Insidious onset with fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness; clients can feel weakness starting, rest, and the strength comes back. Symptoms can be vague.","Eye, face, mouth, throat, neck — Often affected first: drooling, difficulty chewing and swallowing, which affects nutrition and raises aspiration risk.","Respiratory muscles — Weak diaphragm and intercostal muscles can impair the ability to breathe or ventilate appropriately.","Shoulders and hips — Not typically affected, but muscle fatigue after exercise can occur there.","The video (Carrie) — Slow speech, slow blinking, a tracheostomy to help clear secretions, a possibly weak cough, difficulty swallowing."]},{"s":"Myasthenic Crisis▾","a":"myasthenic-crisis","k":["Myasthenic Crisis▾","An acute exacerbation of muscle weakness","Can develop as MG progresses, typically triggered by a stressor: infection, surgery, emotional distress, pregnancy, a menstrual cycle, or inadequate pharmacotherapy or other drugs (not on enough medication, or not taking it appropriately).","In the second video, a simple cold sent Carrie into crisis: difficulty expanding her lungs, difficulty breathing, needing oxygen.","Myasthenic crisis: watch the respiratory muscles. The big concern is respiratory insufficiency, and these clients are at higher risk for respiratory arrest."]},{"s":"Drug Therapy▾","a":"drug-therapy","k":["Drug Therapy▾","Drug therapy is not a cure; the hope is to alter and alleviate symptoms. Several administration routes can be used.","Immunosuppressants","MG is autoimmune, so immunosuppressants, particularly steroids, are used frequently to calm the immune response.","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Cholinesterase is the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine.","Blocking it lets acetylcholine stay in the synaptic junction longer, so it can hopefully find a receptor the antibodies haven't damaged.","They also intensify the effects of the acetylcholine released, hopefully increasing muscle strength.","Neostigmine","A cholinesterase inhibitor (anticholinesterase drug), so essentially a cholinergic drug, used to treat MG symptoms.","Facilitates impulse transmission across the neuromuscular junction, increasing cholinergic action.","At therapeutic levels it affects both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors.","Nicotinic receptors","Adrenal and skeletal muscle.","These are the receptors affected by MG.","Muscarinic receptors","Sweat glands and other glands (and the effects on the GI tract, bladder, heart, lungs and eyes below).","Neostigmine stimulates these too, which causes most of its side effects.","Cholinergic vs Anticholinergic Effects","GI tract — Increased motility, diarrhea — Decreased motility, constipation","Secretions — Increased — Decreased (dries secretions)","Bladder — Urinary urgency — The opposite","Heart rate — Slows — Increases","Lungs — Bronchial constriction — The opposite","Pupils — Miosis (constriction) — The opposite","Use the symptoms to pick the drug: symptomatic bradycardia → an anticholinergic like atropine raises the heart rate. Atropine is also used to dry up secretions, for example for clients on comfort care or hospice who are choking on secretion…","SLUDGE: the cholinergic side effects. Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Diaphoresis, GI distress, Emesis.","Adverse Effects of Neostigmine","Muscarinic stimulation","Increased secretions and GI motility (diarrhea).","Urinary urgency.","Slowed heart rate.","Bronchoconstriction: consider carefully before giving to clients with asthma or other lung disease.","Miosis.","Nicotinic (neuromuscular) stimulation","Therapeutic dose: increased muscle contraction, the desired effect.","Toxic dose: decreased contraction; the overstimulated muscles wear out and can fail, leading to a cholinergic crisis."]},{"s":"Cholinergic Crisis▾","a":"cholinergic-crisis","k":["Cholinergic Crisis▾","Extreme muscle weakness or paralysis, plus signs of excessive muscarinic stimulation: increased intestinal motility (diarrhea, cramping), bradycardia, pupil constriction, lots of saliva, diaphoresis.","It arises from anticholinesterase medications: essentially a drug toxicity, from excessive acetylcholine accumulation.","Risk of respiratory arrest; the client may need mechanical ventilation.","Antidote for the muscarinic signs: atropine (an anticholinergic).","Teach clients with MG to wear a medical alert bracelet at all times."]},{"s":"Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis▾","a":"myasthenic-crisis-vs-cholinergic-crisis","k":["Myasthenic Crisis vs Cholinergic Crisis▾","Both present with respiratory insufficiency and extreme muscle weakness, heading the same direction for opposite reasons.","The problem — Not enough stimulation or acetylcholine — Too much acetylcholine (too much neostigmine)","Why the muscles fail — Too weak, leading to respiratory failure — Overstimulated and worn out, leading to respiratory failure","Typical cause — A stressor, or inadequate medication — Anticholinesterase drug toxicity","After edrophonium — Improves (acetylcholine rises temporarily) — Worsens (already too much acetylcholine)","Edrophonium is a very short-acting cholinesterase inhibitor that raises acetylcholine temporarily. Give it when one of the two crises is suspected: improvement means myasthenic crisis; worsening means cholinergic crisis."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-multiple-sclerosis.html","t":"Multiple Sclerosis","secs":[{"s":"Multiple Sclerosis","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 4","What Multiple Sclerosis Is","Who Gets MS","Pathophysiology","Types of MS","Clinical Manifestations","Goals of Drug Therapy","Disease-Modifying Drugs","Treating an Acute Relapse","Managing MS Symptoms","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the multiple sclerosis voiceover: what MS is, risk factors, the pathophysiology from T cells to axon death, the types of MS, the clinical manifestations, disease-modifying drugs, treatment of an acute relapse, and symptom …"]},{"s":"What Multiple Sclerosis Is▾","a":"what-multiple-sclerosis-is","k":["What Multiple Sclerosis Is▾","Multiple sclerosis (MS)","A chronic, immune-mediated inflammatory disease involving degeneration of CNS myelin (loss of the myelin sheath), scarring (sclerosis or plaque formation) and loss of axons.","A progressive disease, with several types.","Features: lots of inflammation, especially in the white matter of the brain and spinal cord; demyelination of nerve fibers; and scar tissue called gliosis. Glial cells myelinate neurons in the CNS; on imaging, the white spots are areas of s…","Demyelinating disorders damage the myelin sheath and impair neural signal transmission. MS affects the central nervous system; Guillain-Barré affects the peripheral nervous system (a later lecture)."]},{"s":"Who Gets MS▾","a":"who-gets-ms","k":["Who Gets MS▾","The etiology is largely unknown, most likely a combination of genetic predisposition, an immune system problem and an environmental trigger (such as an infection).","Age — Onset typically between 20 and 40.","Sex — More common in females, but males who get MS typically have a more severe, progressive course.","Location — Higher prevalence in moderately cool climates, especially the northern U.S.: above the 40th parallel north and below the 40th parallel south. Areas in between have lower prevalence.","Race — People who are white are more likely to have MS.","Family history — Increases risk.","Possible (associated) — Smoking, vitamin D deficiency, obesity, infection. Not concrete risk factors, but there is evidence of an association."]},{"s":"Pathophysiology▾","a":"pathophysiology","k":["Pathophysiology▾","MS is a diffuse, progressive CNS autoimmune inflammatory disease affecting white and gray matter throughout the brain and spinal cord. The attack targets the myelin sheath and involves T lymphocytes and antibodies.","1 — T lymphocytes migrate to the CNS, cross the blood-brain barrier, and increase inflammation and breakdown of the myelin sheath.","2 — An antigen-antibody reaction in the CNS attacks the myelin, releasing inflammatory mediators.","3 — The oligodendrocytes (myelin-producing cells) are lost.","4 — Axons are demyelinated, and plaque and scar tissue form (sclerotic lesions).","5 — The axons die: nerve signals are first delayed, then lost. Neurons die, and the brain atrophies overall.","Normal — An axon wrapped in an intact myelin sheath. — Quick, almost instantaneous transmission.","Inflamed (early) — The nerve fiber is inflamed but intact. — Still transmitted, maybe a little slower; the client may notice some weakness.","Detached — The myelin detaches and the underlying axon is damaged; scarring and axon death follow. — Completely blocked: a permanent loss of function.","By diagnosis, clients are usually quite symptomatic. Looking back, many realize they'd had weakness much longer than they thought, but brushed it off until they couldn't ignore it."]},{"s":"Types of MS▾","a":"types-of-ms","k":["Types of MS▾","Most types have exacerbations (relapses or flares): a temporary worsening of symptoms, mild or serious, lasting several days to several weeks, which may improve and may be followed by progressively worsening symptoms.","Benign MS — Exacerbations, but after each flare the client returns to baseline: no loss of function.","Relapsing-remitting (RRMS) — The most common type, about 80–90% of cases. Long remissions with a few exacerbations; pretty stable between them, with minimal disability or progression. They don't necessarily return fully to baseline: a littl…","Primary progressive (PPMS) — Gradual, steady progression from the start, with no periods of remission or exacerbation (no flare-ups).","Secondary progressive (SPMS) — Starts with relapses and remissions, with some loss of function each time, then shifts to a more progressive course without remissions.","Progressive relapsing (PRMS) — Gradual progression of symptoms with exacerbations, and fewer remissions overall.","The type is determined by how often exacerbations happen and how long remissions last.","Relapses vary: for about 20% of people they last less than a week; for others 1 to 2 weeks, or 2 weeks to a month.","If relapses get shorter after starting treatment (e.g., from a month down to a week or less), that suggests the medication is working."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"clinical-manifestations","k":["Clinical Manifestations▾","No finding is unique to MS, but some are highly characteristic. The big takeaway: symptoms depend on which nerves are affected.","Common initial symptoms","Paresthesias of the face, trunk and limbs; numbness, tingling, weakness, loss of muscle function.","Optic neuritis and unilateral vision loss.","Impaired gait.","Urinary incontinence.","Some develop seizures.","Symptoms to monitor closely","Cognitive \"cog fog\": difficulty thinking clearly and concentrating, not necessarily a memory problem.","Vision problems.","Depression, related to loss of function.","Fatigue: needs more rest periods.","Pain: chronic (continuous) or acute (with exacerbations); burning or stabbing.","Bowel: constipation most common, possibly incontinence. Bladder: incontinence or retention.","Sexual dysfunction; muscle stiffness or spasms.","Walking and balance issues, vertigo: high fall risk; some eventually need a wheelchair.","Clients with MS are at high risk for falls. Most have walking and balance issues, and some have vertigo."]},{"s":"Goals of Drug Therapy▾","a":"goals-of-drug-therapy","k":["Goals of Drug Therapy▾","There is no cure. Goals: slow disability and keep the client functioning at the highest possible capacity.","Decrease relapse frequency, lengthen remissions, and reduce new brain lesions (which lead to more dysfunction and symptoms).","Three jobs: modify the disease process, treat acute exacerbations, and manage symptoms."]},{"s":"Disease-Modifying Drugs▾","a":"disease-modifying-drugs","k":["Disease-Modifying Drugs▾","Injectables","Interferon beta (Avonex) — Interferons occur naturally in the immune system and can be pro- or anti-inflammatory. Here they keep pro-inflammatory T cells from crossing the blood-brain barrier, reducing inflammation. Decreases relapse rates …","Glatiramer acetate (Copaxone) — Increases anti-inflammatory T cells, which cross the blood-brain barrier and suppress inflammation. Efficacy similar to interferons. — Injection site reactions. A reaction right after injection: flushing, pal…","Oral Drugs","Fingolimod — Retains lymphocytes in the lymph nodes so they don't migrate across the blood-brain barrier, decreasing brain inflammation. Commonly used for RRMS.","Dimethyl fumarate — Developed specifically for MS. Thought to inhibit immune cells, decreasing inflammation and preventing nerve damage.","Infusions","Natalizumab — Keeps circulating T cells from leaving the blood vessels, so they can't cross the blood-brain barrier. Very effective: reduces relapses by up to 68%. Also used for Crohn's disease. Monotherapy only; used for RRMS. — Most commo…","Alemtuzumab — Reserved for poor response to two or more MS drugs: a last effort, not first-line. — Not described in the lecture.","Mitoxantrone — For secondary progressive MS, progressive relapsing MS, and worsening RRMS that never gets a complete remission. — Not described in the lecture.","Natalizumab and PML: only through the TOUCH program, only as monotherapy, and the PML risk is significantly higher with other immunosuppressants like steroids."]},{"s":"Treating an Acute Relapse▾","a":"treating-an-acute-relapse","k":["Treating an Acute Relapse▾","High-dose IV glucocorticoids (\"pulse-dose steroids\") — MS is autoimmune, so very high doses calm the immune response: about 500 mg to 1 g daily for 3 to 5 days (vs 40 to 60 mg for asthma). Not for long-term or frequent use; usually reserved…","IV gamma globulin (IVIG) — For clients who can't tolerate high-dose glucocorticoids.","ACTH (H.P. Acthar Gel) — An adrenocorticotropic hormone gel with prolonged ACTH release after injection. Mechanism not clear, but it treats acute relapses. For clients who can't tolerate glucocorticoids, or when steroids aren't (or weren't)…","Plasmapheresis — A dialysis-like process: blood is removed through a catheter, the plasma is separated from the red cells and replaced with donor plasma, and the blood is reinfused."]},{"s":"Managing MS Symptoms▾","a":"managing-ms-symptoms","k":["Managing MS Symptoms▾","Symptoms vary from client to client, so medications are chosen based on the presentation.","Urinary frequency — An anticholinergic.","Urinary retention — A cholinergic.","Constipation — Hydration and fiber first, then a bulk-forming laxative.","Fatigue — Amantadine, plus teaching to plan the day around rest periods.","Muscle spasms — Muscle relaxants.","Severe cognitive dysfunction (cog fog) — Donepezil (Aricept), normally an Alzheimer's medication."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 3","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics","The Four Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 3's four topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week …"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-four-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Four Topics▾","Five voiceovers, four pages: the Parkinson's lecture and the Parkinson's pharmacology lecture share one page.","The Episodic Headache — Topic 1, Migraines (all of it) — A neurologic disorder that comes and goes in four phases. Neurotransmitter release triggers vasodilation, so the treatment logic follows: triptans constrict the vessels, which is also…","The Neurotransmitter Imbalance — Topic 2, Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology (the disease sections first, then the drug sections) — The substantia nigra stops making dopamine, leaving acetylcholine unopposed, so movement loses i…","The Nerve–Muscle Connection — Topic 3, Myasthenia Gravis (all of it) — Acetylcholine again, but this time the problem is the receptor: antibodies destroy the nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction. The fix is more acetylcholine (…","The Nerve Itself — Topic 4, Multiple Sclerosis (all of it) — Autoimmune again, but now the target is CNS myelin. Inflammation slows the signal; axon death blocks it permanently. The drugs mostly keep T cells from crossing the blood-brain ba…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. Read Parkinson's before myasthenia gravis (MG builds on the acetylcholine review from Parkinson's), and the Parkinson's disease sections before the drug sections."]},{"s":"The Four Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-four-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Four Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Migraines — A 4- to 72-hour headache meeting the diagnostic criteria (at least two pain features plus at least one of nausea and vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia), moving through premonitory, aura, headache and recovery phases. Treat …","2 — Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology — Dopamine loss with relative acetylcholine excess gives the triad: rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, and bradykinesia (the major cause of disability), plus shuffling gait and postural instab…","3 — Myasthenia Gravis — Antibodies destroy nicotinic ACh receptors, causing fluctuating weakness that improves with rest, starting in the eyes and face and threatening swallowing and breathing. Steroids and neostigmine treat it. A myastheni…","4 — Multiple Sclerosis — A T-cell and antibody attack on CNS myelin, ending in axon death and permanent loss. Mostly relapsing-remitting; symptoms depend on which nerves are hit (paresthesias, optic neuritis, cog fog, fatigue, bladder probl…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","Acetylcholine — Parkinson's: \"Dopamine, Acetylcholine & the Imbalance\" (excitatory; in PD it's in relative excess) — Myasthenia Gravis: there's enough ACh but its receptors are destroyed; neostigmine keeps ACh around longer; a cholinergic c…","Anticholinergics — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Cholinergic vs Anticholinergic Effects\" (atropine: faster heart rate, dried secretions, the crisis antidote) — Parkinson's: anticholinergic drugs as one treatment strategy, and anticholinergics push le…","Cholinergics — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Neostigmine\" and \"Adverse Effects of Neostigmine\" (SLUDGE) — Multiple Sclerosis: a cholinergic for urinary retention.","Autoimmunity and steroids — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Drug Therapy\" (immunosuppressants, particularly steroids) and Multiple Sclerosis: \"Treating an Acute Relapse\" (pulse-dose IV steroids) — Both are autoimmune with unknown triggers and a possibl…","Antiemetics — Migraines: \"Abortive (Symptomatic) Therapy\" (ondansetron, promethazine for severe nausea and vomiting) — Parkinson's: some antiemetics (and antidepressants) are the most common cause of secondary parkinsonism.","Hormones and pregnancy — Migraines: \"Risk Factors & Triggers\" (estrogen and progesterone; fewer migraines in pregnancy and menopause) — Parkinson's: decreased estrogen (e.g., hysterectomy) is a risk factor. Myasthenia Gravis: symptoms often…","Swallowing and aspiration — Parkinson's: \"Bradykinesia\" and \"Complications\" (drooling, aspiration pneumonia) — Myasthenia Gravis: difficulty chewing and swallowing raises aspiration risk and affects nutrition.","Respiratory failure — Myasthenia Gravis: \"Myasthenic Crisis\" and \"Cholinergic Crisis\" — Both crises end in respiratory failure for opposite reasons, and both may need mechanical ventilation.","Orthostatic hypotension — Parkinson's: Sinemet, Duopa, and pramipexole with levodopa (\"profound\") — The same teaching point every time: change positions slowly.","Depression — Parkinson's: a risk factor and a complication — Migraines: the recovery phase. Multiple Sclerosis: common with loss of function, and an interferon side effect.","Fatigue and sleep — Parkinson's: fatigue from sleep disturbance; Duopa and pramipexole sleep attacks — Migraines: fatigue and sleep disturbance as triggers and symptoms. Multiple Sclerosis: fatigue managed with amantadine and rest planning.","Falls and balance — Parkinson's: \"Postural Instability & Gait\" — Multiple Sclerosis: walking and balance issues and vertigo; a high fall risk.","Dementia and cognition — Parkinson's: about 40% develop dementia (Lewy bodies) — Multiple Sclerosis: cog fog, treated with donepezil (an Alzheimer's drug) when severe."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the four pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any …","Migraines — No triptans with ischemic stroke, heart disease or angina (they constrict blood vessels). Rimegepant is a CYP substrate: caution with inhibitors and inducers.","Parkinson's Disease & Parkinson's Pharmacology — Aspiration, UTI, skin breakdown and fall risk as mobility declines. Sinemet: skin assessment before starting (melanoma), avoid high-protein meals, change positions slowly, monitor cardiac fun…","Myasthenia Gravis — Myasthenic crisis threatens the respiratory muscles (risk of respiratory arrest). Neostigmine causes bronchoconstriction: caution in asthma or lung disease. Cholinergic crisis means respiratory arrest risk and possible m…","Multiple Sclerosis — High fall risk. Natalizumab: PML (possibly fatal), TOUCH program only, monotherapy only, higher risk with steroids; monitor LFTs and watch for allergic reactions. Glatiramer: monitor severe laryngeal constriction after …","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"Balance is the whole story.\" Parkinson's is too little dopamine for the acetylcholine; myasthenia gravis is too few receptors for the acetylcholine; a cholinergic crisis is too much acetylcholine. Treatment pushes the balance back, and pus…","\"The fix becomes the problem.\" Levodopa loses effect and brings dyskinesias and psychosis; neostigmine overstimulates into cholinergic crisis; triptans constrict vessels a heart patient can't afford to lose; natalizumab suppresses the immun…","\"Autoimmune means steroids, and steroids have limits.\" Myasthenia gravis and MS are both autoimmune with unknown triggers; both use steroids to calm the immune response, and the MS page spells out why they're kept short.","\"Watch the breathing and the swallowing.\" Myasthenia gravis weakens the respiratory and swallowing muscles, both crises end in respiratory failure, and Parkinson's leads to aspiration pneumonia. Airway and nutrition run through all of them.","\"Insidious onset, easy to brush off.\" MG starts as \"a little weakness,\" MS clients look back and realize the weakness started long ago, and Parkinson's takes a while to diagnose because its onset is insidious."]}]},{"f":"week4-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 4","Part 1A — Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)","Part 1B — Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)","Part 2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases","Part 3A — Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","Part 3B — Stroke & Ischemic Stroke","Part 3C — Hemorrhagic Stroke & Intracranial Bleeds","Part 3D — Stroke Treatment & Consequences","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 4: the three topic pages that follow, in the order they were taught. Topic 1: ALS and Guillain-Barré syndrome (Parts 1A–1B). Topic 2: neuro infectious diseases (Part 2)…"]},{"s":"Part 1A — Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)▾","a":"part-1a-amyotrophic-lateral-sclerosis-als","k":["Part 1A — Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)▾","Topic 1, first half: what ALS is, its risk factors, the motor neuron pathology and the excitotoxicity (glutamate) hypothesis, the clinical manifestations, and riluzole.","ALS: What It Is","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)","Also called Lou Gehrig's disease (after the Yankees baseball player who had it).","A neurodegenerative, progressive muscle weakness. The cause is unknown.","It is fatal, usually from respiratory failure caused by the progressive muscle weakness. Death usually occurs about 3 years after diagnosis.","Age — Typically diagnosed between 40 and 70; peaks around 60 to 69.","Sex — More diagnoses in males.","Genetics — A familial form with a genetic mutation accounts for about 10% of diagnoses.","Smoking — Some research suggests it is a risk factor.","ALS: Pathology & Cause","The principal feature is degeneration of the upper and lower motor neurons, so the voluntary muscles are affected.","Large motor neurons decrease in the spinal cord, brainstem and cerebral cortex, followed by degeneration of the remaining motor neurons.","Motor neuron death leads to axonal degeneration, demyelination and sclerosis (scarring). Damaged motor neurons stop sending messages, so the muscles can't function.","The excitotoxicity hypothesis (the current working hypothesis): excessive levels of glutamate, an excitatory neurotransmitter, start a series of events that eventually kill the neuron. It came from finding elevated glutamate in the CSF of p…","ALS: Clinical Manifestations","Signs vary greatly from person to person, depending on which neurons are affected. Initial symptoms relate to upper motor neuron dysfunction, lower motor neuron dysfunction, or both.","Spinal form (about half) — Weakness begins in the arms and legs and progresses to muscle atrophy: spasticity, lost dexterity, impaired gait, trouble walking, tripping and falling, hand weakness.","As it progresses — Difficulty chewing, swallowing and speaking; cognitive and behavioral changes in some; constipation (immobility, diet, muscle weakness); sleep problems.","Eventually — Breathing becomes the main issue: ALS paralyzes the breathing muscles, and respiratory failure is why people with ALS die.","ALS: Riluzole","Status — The only FDA-approved drug for ALS.","How it works — A glutamate inhibitor: blocks glutamate receptor sites and helps reduce damage to the motor neurons.","Benefit — Prolongs survival and slows the deterioration of function, but not by long: about 6 months to a year at most.","Side effects — Dizziness, GI upset, nausea, vomiting. Hepatotoxic: watch liver function."]},{"s":"Part 1B — Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)▾","a":"part-1b-guillain-barr-syndrome-gbs","k":["Part 1B — Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS)▾","Topic 1, second half: what GBS is and what triggers it, the ascending pattern and its timing, the manifestations, and treatment and prognosis.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is","Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), also called acute ascending polyneuritis","A rare autoimmune disorder that causes demyelination: autoantibodies damage the myelin sheath.","Myelin covers the axons and lets impulses travel almost instantaneously. As it is destroyed, impulses are delayed or completely absent.","Viral infection — The usual trigger, a few days to weeks earlier, typically after a respiratory tract or GI infection.","Bacterial infection — Some cases; particularly Campylobacter jejuni, a foodborne illness.","Surgery — Occasionally, days to weeks after surgery.","Flu vaccination — Rarely.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations","The ascending pattern","Symptoms usually start in the lower extremities (weakness and tingling, pins and needles) and ascend up the body.","Recovery goes the opposite direction: strength returns from the top down (upper body, lungs, then the legs).","Severity ranges from tingling and weakness to full leg paralysis, complete quadriplegia, and respiratory insufficiency.","Timing","Most worsen over hours to weeks.","Some decline rapidly, over 24 hours; these clients may end up intubated on a ventilator.","Recovery is just as variable; some need a tracheostomy after weeks of ventilation.","The concern is the ascending weakness reaching the breathing muscles. Once they're affected, GBS can cause respiratory insufficiency and ultimately respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation.","Manifestations","Uncoordinated movements, loss of strength, difficulty walking: a high fall risk.","Decreased sensation, so clients may not notice injury.","Loss of bowel and bladder control.","Blurred vision, which doesn't always fit the ascending pattern and can be an early sign.","Problems breathing, swallowing and chewing, so an aspiration risk.","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis","No cure. The goal is to manage severity and speed recovery.","Acute phase: steroids (high dose, titrated down as the client recovers) and IV immunoglobulin (IVIG), which shorten the severity and duration of symptoms; high-dose IVIG can shorten hospital stays.","Most people get GBS only once; some have recurrences.","Survive — 95%","Return to baseline function — 75%","Residual weakness after about 3 years — 25 to 30%; may never return to baseline","Relapses of weakness and tingling many years later — About 3%"]},{"s":"Part 2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases▾","a":"part-2-neuro-infectious-diseases","k":["Part 2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases▾","Topic 2: meningitis (the meninges, causes, who gets it and how it spreads, pathophysiology, signs and the Kernig and Brudzinski tests, bacterial vs viral, treatment and prevention), encephalitis, and brain abscess.","Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It","The lecture defines meningitis as \"an inflammation of the brain or the spinal cord.\" It is primarily an infection of the pia mater and subarachnoid space, the ventricular system and the CSF.","Infectious meningitis is caused by bacteria: from an infection elsewhere (such as pneumonia), the bloodstream, or a penetrating wound that lets bacteria reach the CSF or brain.","Most common pathogens in adults: Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococci) and Neisseria meningitidis (meningococci).","It may also be caused by viruses (enteroviruses in particular), fungi, parasites or toxins, but bacteria are the most common.","Pia mater — The layer closest to the brain.","Arachnoid mater — The middle layer; the subarachnoid space is filled with cerebrospinal fluid.","Dura mater — The outer layer: really tough and fibrous.","Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads","When and who","Typically fall or winter, when people stay inside with the windows closed.","Typically follows otitis or sinusitis (an ear or sinus infection), an immunocompromised state, or pneumonia.","Pneumococcal meningitis mostly affects young people and those older than 40 (as stated in the lecture).","Outbreaks in college dorms, prisons, military bases: anywhere large groups live and sleep together. It is very communicable.","How it spreads","Respiratory droplets, or contact with contaminated saliva or respiratory secretions.","Some carriers of meningococcal bacteria never develop the disease but can still pass it on.","Meningitis: Pathophysiology","1 — Meningococci or pneumococci are inhaled and attach to the epithelial cells of the nasopharynx.","2 — They cross the mucosal barrier and enter the bloodstream.","3 — They travel to the brain, cross the blood-brain barrier and infect the meninges, starting in the arachnoid mater and the CSF of the subarachnoid space.","4 — Large numbers of neutrophils are recruited; the inflammation causes brain edema, and the exudate thickens the CSF and can impair its flow.","5 — Increased intracranial pressure: an adult's skull is a closed system with no room for more.","Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations","The classic triad: Fever (infection), Headache (meningeal irritation; often throbbing), and Stiff neck / nuchal rigidity (tucking the chin to the chest is painful because it stretches the inflamed meninges).","Other manifestations","Change in mental status.","Nausea and vomiting (can happen with increased ICP).","Severe photophobia.","Decreased consciousness, up to coma or death, from increased ICP.","Seizures in about 30% of cases.","Specific to meningococcus: a petechial or purpuric rash covering the skin and mucous membranes.","Tests for Meningeal Irritation","Kernig sign — Client supine; one leg extended, the other flexed 90° at the hip and 90° at the knee. Support the heel and knee of the bent leg and try to straighten it toward the ceiling. — Pain (the client may even flex the neck).","Brudzinski sign — Client supine; place a hand under the base of the skull and flex the neck. — Neck flexion causes the hips and knees to flex, to reduce the pain of meningeal irritation.","Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis","Bacterial","More common, with a higher fatality rate if treatment isn't started quickly: it can kill within hours.","The petechial rash is associated with this form. It is non-blanchable: press a glass over it and a petechial or purpuric rash won't fade.","Can cause septic emboli: tiny microemboli that lodge in capillaries and block blood flow, so adults and children can lose fingers or limbs.","Long-term effects: hearing loss, seizures, brain damage.","Viral (acute viral meningitis)","The milder form.","Often no elevated white cell count in the CSF on lumbar puncture.","Typically no long-term effects.","Bacterial meningitis: give antibiotics as soon as possible and do not hold them for any reason. It can kill within a matter of hours.","Treating & Preventing Meningitis","IV antibiotics — Aggressive therapy with multiple IV antibiotics; two of the most common are ceftriaxone and vancomycin (vancomycin for a MRSA form).","Acyclovir — Given frequently to prevent herpetic encephalitis, which is deadly, in case the cause is viral. Stopped once the CSF results show it isn't viral.","Steroids — To reduce inflammation.","Anti-seizure medications — Often during the acute phase of meningitis and encephalitis, because increased ICP and inflammation raise seizure risk.","Vaccines (the best prophylaxis) — Available for meningococcus, pneumococcus and H. influenzae. The meningitis vaccine is first given around age 11 or 12, with a booster around 16; a different version is given to people who are immunocomprom…","Encephalitis","Inflammation of the brain tissue","An acute condition, usually caused by a virus.","Causes: West Nile virus (from mosquitoes; the lecture mentions a 2016 outbreak), measles, chickenpox, mumps. It can happen very rapidly, and patients can die.","Herpes simplex virus 1 causes herpetic encephalitis: very severe, with rapidly appearing manifestations, unlike viral meningitis, which is relatively mild.","With severe symptoms, clients are often started on both antibiotics and acyclovir until the cause is known.","Onset — Signs typically appear about 2 to 3 days after infection, once enough virus builds up.","Manifestations — From mild up to mental status changes and coma; fever and headache, nausea and vomiting; CNS changes such as seizures (common); others depending on where the infection is. These are vague and nonspecific; you don't need to …","Acyclovir — For the HSV type in particular, but not effective on other viruses. In herpetic encephalitis it reduces mortality but doesn't necessarily reduce neurological complications.","Supportive care — Fluids, acetaminophen, antiemetics for vomiting, and often anti-seizure medications.","Brain Abscess","What it is — An accumulation of pus within the brain tissue, from a localized infection (like encephalitis) or a systemic infection.","Common sources — Ear, tooth, mastoid or sinus infections, because of their proximity to the brain.","Major culprits — Streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus, bacteria found on the skin that typically cause ear, tooth and sinus infections.","Imaging — The abscess walls are visible, and the midline can be shifted by the pressure from pus and inflammation.","Treatment — Drain the abscess to reduce pressure (the brain is a closed system, so this lowers the risk of secondary injury from swelling), then put antibiotics into the area.","Manifestations — Similar to meningitis and encephalitis: headache, fever, nausea, vomiting; signs of increased ICP (drowsiness, confusion, up to seizures). Other symptoms depend on location; for example, a temporal lobe abscess causes visua…"]},{"s":"Part 3A — Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","a":"part-3a-transient-ischemic-attack-tia","k":["Part 3A — Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","Topic 3, first voiceover (TIA): what a TIA is, carotid vs vertebrobasilar presentations, the Bell's palsy mimic, the key terms, and TIA diagnosis and treatment.","Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","A type of cerebrovascular disease: a focal, acute cerebral insufficiency. Usually only one vessel gets closed off, clotted off or has a spasm, so only that area of the brain is affected.","Typically lasts less than 24 hours: a transient episode of neurologic dysfunction with no acute infarction. Symptoms are often gone by the time the client reaches the ED, with no residual effects.","A stroke, by contrast, causes neurological deficits that last longer than 24 hours.","Fairly common; seen more often in males.","After a TIA, the client is at increased risk for a stroke, especially within the first month. Monitor and teach them to go to the ED if they suspect another TIA, because it might be a full-blown stroke.","Presentation depends on which vessel is involved (a vessel supplying the speech area causes difficulty forming words). Recurrent TIAs usually produce the same symptoms each time. Onset is abrupt, and recovery is abrupt and rapid.","The lecture also says a CT can show blood in the brain tissue with a TIA, though \"not as clear as it is with an ischemic stroke.\" That is kept as stated; check the slide wording.","Carotid area","Weakness or heaviness in the arms, legs or face.","Numbness.","Vision loss.","Vertebrobasilar area (back of the brain)","Blurry vision.","Vertigo.","Ataxia.","Motor or sensory changes.","TIA mimic: Bell's palsy","A problem with the facial nerve causes facial paralysis (drooping mouth, drooping face), so these clients are often evaluated for a stroke or TIA. Bell's palsy recovers, but it takes time and requires treatment; a TIA recovers much more qui…","Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral","Dysphagia (with a G) — Difficulty swallowing. Memory trick: G for gastric. — Keep NPO: high risk for aspiration (a foreign substance entering the lungs from eating and drinking without swallowing properly), which can inflame and damage the …","Dysphasia (with an S) — Difficulty with speech. — A communication problem.","Contralateral — Crossing the midline: the opposite side. — Manifestations appear on the opposite side of the body from the brain damage.","Ipsilateral — Staying on the same side. — Manifestations appear on the same side as the damage.","TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment","History and physical — Is there a pattern of other vascular problems?","Quick CT scan first — Rule out hemorrhage, which is easy to see on CT. Then look for other evidence of CVA, such as lacunar infarcts (tiny infarcts that may cause no symptoms).","MRA (MRI with angiography) — Uses dye to highlight aneurysms.","Carotid Doppler studies — Look for buildup in the carotid arteries reducing blood flow to the brain.","Echocardiogram — Look for a cardiac cause, such as a clot on the left side of the heart that could break off and travel to the brain.","Treatment depends on the cause; the goal is reducing the risk of a full stroke","Small doses of aspirin.","Clopidogrel (Plavix): an antiplatelet that keeps platelets from sticking together.","If the TIA is cardiac-related: heparin or warfarin to prevent coagulation.","If caused by carotid narrowing or stenosis: carotid endarterectomy (opens the artery by removing the plaque), or carotid angioplasty (a balloon catheter fed up through usually the femoral artery and inflated, with a filter to catch plaque p…","Education on lifestyle changes, especially the modifiable risk factors for stroke."]},{"s":"Part 3B — Stroke & Ischemic Stroke▾","a":"part-3b-stroke-ischemic-stroke","k":["Part 3B — Stroke & Ischemic Stroke▾","Topic 3, second voiceover (Stroke and hemorrhage 1): stroke types and statistics, risk factors, signs and the first CT, ischemic stroke (thrombotic and embolic), and the penumbra.","Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics","Also called a cerebrovascular accident (CVA), the term used in hospitals, or a \"brain attack\" (a term that didn't stick).","The result of a problem with blood supply to the brain. Two types: ischemic, about 87% of strokes, and hemorrhagic, about 13%.","About 800,000 strokes a year; for over half a million people it's their first stroke. Risk rises with age, but about a third of people with a first stroke are under 65.","A leading cause of disability. Third leading cause of death in women, fifth in men, fourth overall. Someone has a stroke about every 40 seconds and someone dies from one every 4 minutes; about $36.5 billion in healthcare costs a year.","Stroke Risk Factors","Hypertension — Especially uncontrolled or undiagnosed. Commonly leads to hemorrhagic stroke, often by way of an aneurysm that ruptures, or by weakening the arterial wall until it ruptures. More than doubles the risk of intracranial hemorrha…","Atherosclerosis and hyperlipidemia — Vessel damage leads to plaque formation and clots, in brain vessels as in the heart.","Tobacco use — Damages the endothelial lining of blood vessels.","Diabetes — Damages blood vessel walls, which can lead to plaque formation.","Oral contraceptives — Particularly estrogen and progesterone: increase plasma fibrinogen and coagulation factor activity.","Age (starred) — Advanced age: higher risk of stroke but a better chance of recovery, because brain atrophy leaves more room in the closed skull for swelling and bleeding.","Male sex — Males are at higher risk overall.","Sickle cell anemia — In crisis, blood cells clump and can lodge in brain vessels.","Atrial fibrillation — Quivering atria let blood pool and clot; the clot can travel to the brain. Often treated with anticoagulants such as warfarin, which bring their own bleeding risk.","Signs of a Stroke & the First Test","Clinical manifestations","Numbness or weakness of the face, arm or leg, usually on one side (asymmetrical weakness).","Sudden confusion; difficulty speaking or understanding speech; slurred speech (dysarthria).","Vision trouble in one or both eyes.","Trouble walking, balance problems, dizziness, lack of coordination (ataxia).","A sudden, severe headache with no known cause, particularly important in hemorrhagic stroke.","CT without contrast, done very quickly","Part of the stroke alert protocol (with attention to airway and breathing as needed); it drives the rest of the treatment plan.","Blood shows up right away as white; an ischemic stroke shows as a darkened area.","We must know ischemic vs hemorrhagic: never anticoagulate or give a thrombolytic to a hemorrhagic stroke. It makes the bleeding worse and could kill the client.","Ischemic Stroke","An occlusion of a cerebral artery by a thrombus or an embolus; everything distal to the occlusion loses its blood supply.","Both types typically present the same way, based on which part of the brain isn't perfused, but the etiology guides treatment: atherosclerosis? a hypercoagulability problem? a thrombus or embolism elsewhere?","On CT, a darkened area that isn't prominent at first but becomes more noticeable if blood flow isn't restored.","A tiny vessel occlusion may go unnoticed (evidence of an old stroke is sometimes found on an unrelated CT). A larger vessel occlusion produces symptoms based on its location.","Neuro deficits start within about one minute of oxygen deprivation; without reestablished blood flow, the damage becomes irreversible.","Hemorrhagic conversion: after an ischemic stroke, blood from the peripheral circulation extravasates across a disrupted blood-brain barrier into the brain tissue once reperfusion is established, naturally or as a result of therapy. A hemorr…","Thrombotic — Arterial obstruction from thrombus formation. Atherosclerosis: vessel-lining damage leads to plaque that narrows or blocks the vessel; if the plaque ruptures, a clot forms. Hypercoagulable states: cancer, oral contraceptives, a…","Embolic — Look at the heart first: Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain. Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from po…","Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain.","Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from pooled blood. Usually arterial clots, on the left side of the heart.","Patent foramen ovale: a birth-defect hole in the septum between the atria lets a venous clot (such as a DVT piece) pass from the right atrium to the left atrium instead of going to the lungs as a PE; the left ventricle then sends it to the …","Valve infection, particularly the mitral valve: vegetation forms, and a piece breaks off as an embolus.","Mechanical valve replacement (more than a porcine valve): clots can form around the valve.","Carotid artery plaque can rupture, and the clot travels to the brain.","The lecture describes the mitral valve as \"between the right and left ventricle.\" It is kept out of the table as a likely slip; check the slide.","Assessment clues for embolic risk","Carotid bruit: a swishing sound on auscultation from turbulent flow through a vessel narrowed by a growing plaque.","ECG: in A-fib, little quivering lines replace the P waves (the atria aren't contracting properly). These clients are at higher risk for embolic stroke, especially if newly diagnosed and not yet anticoagulated long enough.","Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra","Dead tissue (core)","Injured beyond repair.","Penumbra","The extended area of injured but salvageable tissue around the core.","Goal for ischemic stroke: treatment within 3 hours of symptom onset, to reestablish blood flow and save the penumbra. Once a stroke protocol is activated, the client goes to CT first (often bypassing a room), labs are drawn quickly and the …"]},{"s":"Part 3C — Hemorrhagic Stroke & Intracranial Bleeds▾","a":"part-3c-hemorrhagic-stroke-intracranial-bleeds","k":["Part 3C — Hemorrhagic Stroke & Intracranial Bleeds▾","Topic 3, third voiceover (Stroke and hemorrhage 2): hemorrhagic stroke, epidural, subdural and subarachnoid bleeds, subarachnoid hemorrhage management, berry aneurysms and AVMs, and BE FAST.","Hemorrhagic Stroke","A ruptured vessel with bleeding within the brain tissue (the parenchyma).","Leading cause: untreated hypertension; over time the pressure weakens vessels until they rupture. Clients with aneurysms are also at increased risk.","About 38% mortality, within minutes to hours; in the brainstem, death can occur within seconds. Mortality depends on the size of the area affected.","Blood is very irritating to brain tissue, so inflammation causes edema; blood plus edema in the confined skull sharply increases ICP. The brain tissue can collapse on itself (herniation), which usually results in death.","Usually ages 30 to 60, younger than ischemic stroke clients. High morbidity and mortality, with serious disabilities.","Prognosis depends on","Age: older clients have brain atrophy and more room, so a better prognosis.","Location and size: the brainstem has a very poor prognosis.","How rapid the bleeding is, and whether it causes brain distortion or shift. A midline shift (a bleed pushing one side across the center line, seen on a CT report) means a very poor prognosis.","Presentation","The worst headache of their life.","A rapid change in level of consciousness.","With subdural, epidural or subarachnoid bleeds, the client presents like meningitis from meningeal irritation: nuchal rigidity and photophobia.","Other Intracranial Hemorrhages","Bleeding in the layers covering the brain, named by location. A bleed in the brain tissue itself is a hemorrhagic stroke.","Epidural — Between the skull and the dura mater. — Usually with skull fractures (think trauma). Tend to be arterial, so even a less severe injury can mean a lot of blood loss.","Subdural — Below the dura. — Tend to be venous, e.g., injury to bridging veins. Severe injury can cause rapid decline, but venous blood may accumulate slowly: a client hits their head, seems fine, and symptoms appear later.","Subarachnoid — Between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, where the CSF lives. — Usually from an aneurysm or an AVM. Causes bloody CSF.","Treatment for epidural and subdural bleeds is to evacuate the collecting blood and relieve pressure on the brain. Blood bleeding into the brain tissue cannot be evacuated.","Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","Diagnosis by lumbar puncture","Normal CSF is clear or slightly yellow. The first tube may have a little blood from needle trauma, but it should clear. With a subarachnoid bleed, the fluid stays bloody. A subarachnoid hemorrhage is identified by lumbar puncture about 12 h…","Bed rest and BP control — Avoid big BP fluctuations: keep it managed to prevent further bleeding, but high enough to perfuse the brain. The nurse is often given very specific BP parameters.","Surgery — If an aneurysm is the culprit, surgical intervention (clipping or coiling), usually done ASAP.","Anticonvulsant — Blood irritates the brain, so seizure risk rises.","Calcium channel blocker — After clipping or coiling, to help treat the symptoms of the subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Triple H — Keeps the brain perfused: pressors to keep BP high enough, colloids and a hypervolemic state, and some expected hemodilution.","The lecture describes the calcium channel blocker as working \"by narrowing and slowing that blood flow.\" It is kept out of the table as stated; check the slide for the intended mechanism.","Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling","Berry aneurysm — The most common aneurysm that ruptures in the brain: little berries hanging off the artery that rupture when big enough.","AVM (arteriovenous malformation) — Normally arteries divide into arterioles, capillaries, venules, then veins. In an AVM the junction is a jumbled clump, so high-pressure arterial blood goes directly into the vein without slowing through ca…","Clipping — A clip is placed around the aneurysm to block blood flow into it.","Coiling (endovascular embolization) — A catheter inserted in the groin is threaded into the aneurysm, and detachable platinum coils reduce blood flow into the sac, lowering the chance of rupture.","The lecture also says \"berry aneurysms are often located in the veins,\" which conflicts with the same slide (\"hanging off the artery\") and with Week 1. It is kept out of the table; check the slide.","BE FAST & Last Known Normal","B: Balance — Headache, dizziness, loss of balance, trouble walking; falls, especially in the elderly.","E: Eyes — Sudden vision loss in one or both eyes; unequal pupils.","F: Face — Facial asymmetry: have them smile. Differentiate from Bell's palsy.","A: Arm — Weakness: both arms held up perpendicular to the body; does one drift?","S: Speech — Difficulty speaking or slurred words.","T: Time — Call 911, get to the hospital, activate a stroke alert.","Find out exactly when symptoms started: the \"last known normal.\" Treatment must happen within a certain time frame."]},{"s":"Part 3D — Stroke Treatment & Consequences▾","a":"part-3d-stroke-treatment-consequences","k":["Part 3D — Stroke Treatment & Consequences▾","Topic 3, fourth voiceover (Stroke treatment): the first steps, hemorrhagic vs ischemic treatment, alteplase, and the consequences of stroke.","Stroke Treatment: First Steps","ABCs: can they maintain their airway, are they breathing, is there a pulse? A GCS below 8 raises concern about airway protection, so the client is intubated. Stroke clients may also have decreased respiratory drive.","Stroke alert / protocol: labs, CT, the neuro team, and a blood sugar check, because hypoglycemia can mimic a stroke.","CT scan without contrast, done first to exclude hemorrhagic stroke: fast, and best in the acute stage. An MRI gives better images later, but it is slower and less available, so it isn't the first choice in an emergency.","Hemorrhagic stroke","Reverse anticoagulation: is there a reversal agent or antidote for the client's medications?","Manage hypertension, the likely cause.","Manage ICP: elevate the head of the bed, give antipyretics for fever, keep the head in a neutral position.","Ischemic stroke","Thrombolytics (clot busters) to open the occlusion and protect and reperfuse the penumbra.","Mechanical thrombectomy: a surgeon removes the clot manually (also called a penumbra procedure).","Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke","Heparin and antiplatelets don't dissolve a clot; they only prevent one from forming. Fibrinolytics actually break up clots.","Alteplase (tPA, tissue plasminogen activator), the most commonly used fibrinolytic, converts plasminogen to plasmin; plasmin digests the fibrin.","Used in severe thrombotic events: acute MI, PE, ischemic stroke, occasionally femoral artery clots or a clotted central line. Given IV.","Monitor BP — It can cause hypotension.","Monitor HR and rhythm — Arrhythmias can develop with reperfusion.","Bleeding risk — Increased risk of intracranial bleeding. Don't give it to clients at high risk for bleeding (such as a previous hemorrhagic stroke or a GI bleed).","CT first — Always rule out hemorrhagic stroke before giving it.","With heparin — Often given with heparin to prevent new clots from forming.","Antidote — Aminocaproic acid, an antithrombolytic: blocks plasminogen binding and activation to plasmin, reducing fibrinolysis to stop bleeding. Give it only if the hemorrhage is life-threatening.","Consequences of Stroke","Left side of the brain affected","Difficulty with communication (verbal and written) and with more analytical activities.","Right side of the brain affected","Difficulty with things like imagination, music and art.","Dysphagia — A swallow evaluation comes before giving food or advancing a diet. Speech pathology does a barium swallow study under real-time x-ray with different thicknesses of food and liquid, then recommends a diet: regular, thickened liqu…","Motor and sensory deficits — Neglect of body parts no longer getting sensory input; poor coordination (fall risk); loss of muscle groups, which atrophy over time.","Flaccidity → spasticity → contractures — Flaccid (limp, weak) muscles on the contralateral side become spastic (painful), then contractures (permanently shortened muscles, joints stuck in abnormal positions), typically within the first 6 we…","Homonymous hemianopia — Contralateral field blindness: loss (or blurring) of up to half the vision, on the same side of both visual fields.","Aphasia — The global term for affected language: dysphasia (speech), receptive aphasia (understanding), reading and writing problems; one or all.","Depression — Very common, from significant disabilities and loss of independence.","Memory problems — Even well-known things like family members' names.","Behavior changes — Increased emotional response (upset, angry or crying more easily); slower reaction times; hesitance or caution; confusion or disorientation; apathy.","The lecture also says clients \"can underestimate their own abilities\" and calls it a big safety concern, alongside slower reaction times (fall risk). That is kept as stated; check the slide wording."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One row per part: the points to have cold before an exam. Every detail is taught in full in the parts above.","1A. ALS — Lou Gehrig's disease; unknown cause; fatal from respiratory failure, about 3 years after diagnosis. Ages 40–70 (peak 60–69), males, 10% familial, possibly smoking. Upper and lower motor neuron degeneration → voluntary muscles. Exc…","1B. Guillain-Barré Syndrome — Autoimmune demyelination of peripheral nerves (acute ascending polyneuritis). Trigger: viral respiratory or GI infection; also Campylobacter jejuni, surgery, rarely flu vaccine. Weakness and tingling ascend fro…","2. Neuro Infectious Diseases — Meningitis: S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis; respiratory droplets; fall and winter; dorms, prisons, military. Triad: fever, headache, nuchal rigidity; photophobia; seizures in 30%; non-blanching petechial ra…","3A. TIA — Focal, acute, one vessel; under 24 hours, no infarction, no residual effects. Stroke risk highest in the first month. Carotid: weakness, numbness, vision loss. Vertebrobasilar: blurry vision, vertigo, ataxia. Bell's palsy mimic. D…","3B. Stroke & Ischemic Stroke — 87% ischemic, 13% hemorrhagic; leading cause of disability. Risks: HTN, atherosclerosis, tobacco, diabetes, OCs, age (higher risk, better recovery), males, sickle cell, A-fib. Non-contrast CT first: blood is w…","3C. Hemorrhagic Stroke & Bleeds — Ruptured vessel; untreated HTN; 38% mortality; ages 30–60; edema and ICP → herniation. Prognosis: age, location and size, bleeding speed and midline shift. Worst headache of their life; meningeal signs. Epi…","3D. Stroke Treatment & Consequences — ABCs (GCS below 8 → intubate), blood sugar, non-contrast CT first. Hemorrhagic: reverse anticoagulation, manage BP and ICP (HOB up, antipyretics, neutral head). Ischemic: alteplase (plasminogen → plasmi…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the seven parts above: a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week4-als-gbs.html","t":"ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome","secs":[{"s":"ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 1","ALS: What It Is","ALS: Pathology & Cause","ALS: Clinical Manifestations","ALS: Riluzole","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations","Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis","Self-Test Flashcards","This page combines two short voiceovers. ALS: what it is, who gets it, the motor neuron pathology, the excitotoxicity hypothesis, clinical manifestations and riluzole. Guillain-Barré syndrome: the autoimmune demyelination, its triggers, the…"]},{"s":"ALS: What It Is▾","a":"als-what-it-is","k":["ALS: What It Is▾","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)","Also called Lou Gehrig's disease (after the Yankees baseball player who had it).","A neurodegenerative, progressive muscle weakness. The cause is unknown.","It is fatal, usually from respiratory failure caused by the progressive muscle weakness. Death usually occurs about 3 years after diagnosis.","Age — Typically diagnosed between 40 and 70; peaks around 60 to 69.","Sex — More diagnoses in males.","Genetics — A familial form with a genetic mutation accounts for about 10% of diagnoses.","Smoking — Some research suggests it is a risk factor."]},{"s":"ALS: Pathology & Cause▾","a":"als-pathology-cause","k":["ALS: Pathology & Cause▾","The principal feature is degeneration of the upper and lower motor neurons, so the voluntary muscles are affected.","Large motor neurons decrease in the spinal cord, brainstem and cerebral cortex, followed by degeneration of the remaining motor neurons.","Motor neuron death leads to axonal degeneration, demyelination and sclerosis (scarring). Damaged motor neurons stop sending messages, so the muscles can't function.","The excitotoxicity hypothesis (the current working hypothesis): excessive levels of glutamate, an excitatory neurotransmitter, start a series of events that eventually kill the neuron. It came from finding elevated glutamate in the CSF of p…"]},{"s":"ALS: Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"als-clinical-manifestations","k":["ALS: Clinical Manifestations▾","Signs vary greatly from person to person, depending on which neurons are affected. Initial symptoms relate to upper motor neuron dysfunction, lower motor neuron dysfunction, or both.","Spinal form (about half) — Weakness begins in the arms and legs and progresses to muscle atrophy: spasticity, lost dexterity, impaired gait, trouble walking, tripping and falling, hand weakness.","As it progresses — Difficulty chewing, swallowing and speaking; cognitive and behavioral changes in some; constipation (immobility, diet, muscle weakness); sleep problems.","Eventually — Breathing becomes the main issue: ALS paralyzes the breathing muscles, and respiratory failure is why people with ALS die."]},{"s":"ALS: Riluzole▾","a":"als-riluzole","k":["ALS: Riluzole▾","Status — The only FDA-approved drug for ALS.","How it works — A glutamate inhibitor: blocks glutamate receptor sites and helps reduce damage to the motor neurons.","Benefit — Prolongs survival and slows the deterioration of function, but not by long: about 6 months to a year at most.","Side effects — Dizziness, GI upset, nausea, vomiting. Hepatotoxic: watch liver function."]},{"s":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is▾","a":"guillain-barr-syndrome-what-it-is","k":["Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is▾","Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), also called acute ascending polyneuritis","A rare autoimmune disorder that causes demyelination: autoantibodies damage the myelin sheath.","Myelin covers the axons and lets impulses travel almost instantaneously. As it is destroyed, impulses are delayed or completely absent.","CNS demyelination (multiple sclerosis) is covered in Multiple Sclerosis; GBS is the peripheral nervous system version","Viral infection — The usual trigger, a few days to weeks earlier, typically after a respiratory tract or GI infection.","Bacterial infection — Some cases; particularly Campylobacter jejuni, a foodborne illness.","Surgery — Occasionally, days to weeks after surgery.","Flu vaccination — Rarely."]},{"s":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations▾","a":"guillain-barr-syndrome-pattern-manifestations","k":["Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Pattern & Manifestations▾","The ascending pattern","Symptoms usually start in the lower extremities (weakness and tingling, pins and needles) and ascend up the body.","Recovery goes the opposite direction: strength returns from the top down (upper body, lungs, then the legs).","Severity ranges from tingling and weakness to full leg paralysis, complete quadriplegia, and respiratory insufficiency.","Timing","Most worsen over hours to weeks.","Some decline rapidly, over 24 hours; these clients may end up intubated on a ventilator.","Recovery is just as variable; some need a tracheostomy after weeks of ventilation.","The concern is the ascending weakness reaching the breathing muscles. Once they're affected, GBS can cause respiratory insufficiency and ultimately respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation.","Manifestations","Uncoordinated movements, loss of strength, difficulty walking: a high fall risk.","Decreased sensation, so clients may not notice injury.","Loss of bowel and bladder control.","Blurred vision, which doesn't always fit the ascending pattern and can be an early sign.","Problems breathing, swallowing and chewing, so an aspiration risk."]},{"s":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis▾","a":"guillain-barr-syndrome-treatment-prognosis","k":["Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis▾","No cure. The goal is to manage severity and speed recovery.","Acute phase: steroids (high dose, titrated down as the client recovers) and IV immunoglobulin (IVIG), which shorten the severity and duration of symptoms; high-dose IVIG can shorten hospital stays.","Most people get GBS only once; some have recurrences.","Survive — 95%","Return to baseline function — 75%","Residual weakness after about 3 years — 25 to 30%; may never return to baseline","Relapses of weakness and tingling many years later — About 3%"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-neuro-infections.html","t":"Neuro Infectious Diseases","secs":[{"s":"Neuro Infectious Diseases","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 2","Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It","Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads","Meningitis: Pathophysiology","Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations","Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis","Treating & Preventing Meningitis","Encephalitis","Brain Abscess","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the inflammatory and infectious neurological disorders voiceover: meningitis (causes, the meninges, spread, pathophysiology, signs and the Kernig and Brudzinski tests, bacterial vs viral, treatment and prevention), encepha…"]},{"s":"Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It▾","a":"meningitis-what-it-is-what-causes-it","k":["Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It▾","The lecture defines meningitis as \"an inflammation of the brain or the spinal cord.\" It is primarily an infection of the pia mater and subarachnoid space, the ventricular system and the CSF.","Infectious meningitis is caused by bacteria: from an infection elsewhere (such as pneumonia), the bloodstream, or a penetrating wound that lets bacteria reach the CSF or brain.","Most common pathogens in adults: Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococci) and Neisseria meningitidis (meningococci).","It may also be caused by viruses (enteroviruses in particular), fungi, parasites or toxins, but bacteria are the most common.","Pia mater — The layer closest to the brain.","Arachnoid mater — The middle layer; the subarachnoid space is filled with cerebrospinal fluid.","Dura mater — The outer layer: really tough and fibrous."]},{"s":"Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads▾","a":"who-gets-meningitis-how-it-spreads","k":["Who Gets Meningitis & How It Spreads▾","When and who","Typically fall or winter, when people stay inside with the windows closed.","Typically follows otitis or sinusitis (an ear or sinus infection), an immunocompromised state, or pneumonia.","Pneumococcal meningitis mostly affects young people and those older than 40 (as stated in the lecture).","Outbreaks in college dorms, prisons, military bases: anywhere large groups live and sleep together. It is very communicable.","How it spreads","Respiratory droplets, or contact with contaminated saliva or respiratory secretions.","Some carriers of meningococcal bacteria never develop the disease but can still pass it on."]},{"s":"Meningitis: Pathophysiology▾","a":"meningitis-pathophysiology","k":["Meningitis: Pathophysiology▾","1 — Meningococci or pneumococci are inhaled and attach to the epithelial cells of the nasopharynx.","2 — They cross the mucosal barrier and enter the bloodstream.","3 — They travel to the brain, cross the blood-brain barrier and infect the meninges, starting in the arachnoid mater and the CSF of the subarachnoid space.","4 — Large numbers of neutrophils are recruited; the inflammation causes brain edema, and the exudate thickens the CSF and can impair its flow.","5 — Increased intracranial pressure: an adult's skull is a closed system with no room for more."]},{"s":"Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"meningitis-clinical-manifestations","k":["Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations▾","The classic triad: Fever (infection), Headache (meningeal irritation; often throbbing), and Stiff neck / nuchal rigidity (tucking the chin to the chest is painful because it stretches the inflamed meninges).","Other manifestations","Change in mental status.","Nausea and vomiting (can happen with increased ICP).","Severe photophobia.","Decreased consciousness, up to coma or death, from increased ICP.","Seizures in about 30% of cases.","Specific to meningococcus: a petechial or purpuric rash covering the skin and mucous membranes.","Tests for Meningeal Irritation","Kernig sign — Client supine; one leg extended, the other flexed 90° at the hip and 90° at the knee. Support the heel and knee of the bent leg and try to straighten it toward the ceiling. — Pain (the client may even flex the neck).","Brudzinski sign — Client supine; place a hand under the base of the skull and flex the neck. — Neck flexion causes the hips and knees to flex, to reduce the pain of meningeal irritation."]},{"s":"Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis▾","a":"bacterial-vs-viral-meningitis","k":["Bacterial vs Viral Meningitis▾","Bacterial","More common, with a higher fatality rate if treatment isn't started quickly: it can kill within hours.","The petechial rash is associated with this form. It is non-blanchable: press a glass over it and a petechial or purpuric rash won't fade.","Can cause septic emboli: tiny microemboli that lodge in capillaries and block blood flow, so adults and children can lose fingers or limbs.","Long-term effects: hearing loss, seizures, brain damage.","Viral (acute viral meningitis)","The milder form.","Often no elevated white cell count in the CSF on lumbar puncture.","Typically no long-term effects.","Bacterial meningitis: give antibiotics as soon as possible and do not hold them for any reason. It can kill within a matter of hours."]},{"s":"Treating & Preventing Meningitis▾","a":"treating-preventing-meningitis","k":["Treating & Preventing Meningitis▾","IV antibiotics — Aggressive therapy with multiple IV antibiotics; two of the most common are ceftriaxone and vancomycin (vancomycin for a MRSA form).","Acyclovir — Given frequently to prevent herpetic encephalitis, which is deadly, in case the cause is viral. Stopped once the CSF results show it isn't viral.","Steroids — To reduce inflammation.","Anti-seizure medications — Often during the acute phase of meningitis and encephalitis, because increased ICP and inflammation raise seizure risk.","Vaccines (the best prophylaxis) — Available for meningococcus, pneumococcus and H. influenzae. The meningitis vaccine is first given around age 11 or 12, with a booster around 16; a different version is given to people who are immunocomprom…"]},{"s":"Encephalitis▾","a":"encephalitis","k":["Encephalitis▾","Inflammation of the brain tissue","An acute condition, usually caused by a virus.","Causes: West Nile virus (from mosquitoes; the lecture mentions a 2016 outbreak), measles, chickenpox, mumps. It can happen very rapidly, and patients can die.","Herpes simplex virus 1 causes herpetic encephalitis: very severe, with rapidly appearing manifestations, unlike viral meningitis, which is relatively mild.","With severe symptoms, clients are often started on both antibiotics and acyclovir until the cause is known.","Onset — Signs typically appear about 2 to 3 days after infection, once enough virus builds up.","Manifestations — From mild up to mental status changes and coma; fever and headache, nausea and vomiting; CNS changes such as seizures (common); others depending on where the infection is. These are vague and nonspecific; you don't need to …","Acyclovir — For the HSV type in particular, but not effective on other viruses. In herpetic encephalitis it reduces mortality but doesn't necessarily reduce neurological complications.","Supportive care — Fluids, acetaminophen, antiemetics for vomiting, and often anti-seizure medications."]},{"s":"Brain Abscess▾","a":"brain-abscess","k":["Brain Abscess▾","What it is — An accumulation of pus within the brain tissue, from a localized infection (like encephalitis) or a systemic infection.","Common sources — Ear, tooth, mastoid or sinus infections, because of their proximity to the brain.","Major culprits — Streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus, bacteria found on the skin that typically cause ear, tooth and sinus infections.","Imaging — The abscess walls are visible, and the midline can be shifted by the pressure from pus and inflammation.","Treatment — Drain the abscess to reduce pressure (the brain is a closed system, so this lowers the risk of secondary injury from swelling), then put antibiotics into the area.","Manifestations — Similar to meningitis and encephalitis: headache, fever, nausea, vomiting; signs of increased ICP (drowsiness, confusion, up to seizures). Other symptoms depend on location; for example, a temporal lobe abscess causes visua…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-stroke.html","t":"TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage","secs":[{"s":"TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 3","Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)","Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral","TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment","Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics","Stroke Risk Factors","Signs of a Stroke & the First Test","Ischemic Stroke","Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra","Hemorrhagic Stroke","Other Intracranial Hemorrhages","Subarachnoid Hemorrhage","Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling","BE FAST & Last Known Normal","Stroke Treatment: First Steps","Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke","Consequences of Stroke","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows four voiceovers: transient ischemic attacks; stroke and hemorrhage part 1 (stroke types, statistics, risk factors, signs, CT, ischemic stroke and the penumbra); part 2 (hemorrhagic stroke, other intracranial hemorrhages, a…"]},{"s":"Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","a":"transient-ischemic-attack-tia","k":["Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA)▾","A type of cerebrovascular disease: a focal, acute cerebral insufficiency. Usually only one vessel gets closed off, clotted off or has a spasm, so only that area of the brain is affected.","Typically lasts less than 24 hours: a transient episode of neurologic dysfunction with no acute infarction. Symptoms are often gone by the time the client reaches the ED, with no residual effects.","A stroke, by contrast, causes neurological deficits that last longer than 24 hours.","Fairly common; seen more often in males.","After a TIA, the client is at increased risk for a stroke, especially within the first month. Monitor and teach them to go to the ED if they suspect another TIA, because it might be a full-blown stroke.","Presentation depends on which vessel is involved (a vessel supplying the speech area causes difficulty forming words). Recurrent TIAs usually produce the same symptoms each time. Onset is abrupt, and recovery is abrupt and rapid.","The lecture also says a CT can show blood in the brain tissue with a TIA, though \"not as clear as it is with an ischemic stroke.\" That is kept as stated; check the slide wording.","Carotid area","Weakness or heaviness in the arms, legs or face.","Numbness.","Vision loss.","Vertebrobasilar area (back of the brain)","Blurry vision.","Vertigo.","Ataxia.","Motor or sensory changes.","TIA mimic: Bell's palsy","A problem with the facial nerve causes facial paralysis (drooping mouth, drooping face), so these clients are often evaluated for a stroke or TIA. Bell's palsy recovers, but it takes time and requires treatment; a TIA recovers much more qui…"]},{"s":"Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral▾","a":"key-terms-dysphagia-dysphasia-contralateral-ipsilateral","k":["Key Terms: Dysphagia, Dysphasia, Contralateral & Ipsilateral▾","Dysphagia (with a G) — Difficulty swallowing. Memory trick: G for gastric. — Keep NPO: high risk for aspiration (a foreign substance entering the lungs from eating and drinking without swallowing properly), which can inflame and damage the …","Dysphasia (with an S) — Difficulty with speech. — A communication problem.","Contralateral — Crossing the midline: the opposite side. — Manifestations appear on the opposite side of the body from the brain damage.","Ipsilateral — Staying on the same side. — Manifestations appear on the same side as the damage."]},{"s":"TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment▾","a":"tia-diagnosis-treatment","k":["TIA: Diagnosis & Treatment▾","History and physical — Is there a pattern of other vascular problems?","Quick CT scan first — Rule out hemorrhage, which is easy to see on CT. Then look for other evidence of CVA, such as lacunar infarcts (tiny infarcts that may cause no symptoms).","MRA (MRI with angiography) — Uses dye to highlight aneurysms.","Carotid Doppler studies — Look for buildup in the carotid arteries reducing blood flow to the brain.","Echocardiogram — Look for a cardiac cause, such as a clot on the left side of the heart that could break off and travel to the brain.","Treatment depends on the cause; the goal is reducing the risk of a full stroke","Small doses of aspirin.","Clopidogrel (Plavix): an antiplatelet that keeps platelets from sticking together.","If the TIA is cardiac-related: heparin or warfarin to prevent coagulation.","If caused by carotid narrowing or stenosis: carotid endarterectomy (opens the artery by removing the plaque), or carotid angioplasty (a balloon catheter fed up through usually the femoral artery and inflated, with a filter to catch plaque p…","Education on lifestyle changes, especially the modifiable risk factors for stroke."]},{"s":"Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics▾","a":"stroke-cva-overview-statistics","k":["Stroke (CVA): Overview & Statistics▾","Also called a cerebrovascular accident (CVA), the term used in hospitals, or a \"brain attack\" (a term that didn't stick).","The result of a problem with blood supply to the brain. Two types: ischemic, about 87% of strokes, and hemorrhagic, about 13%.","About 800,000 strokes a year; for over half a million people it's their first stroke. Risk rises with age, but about a third of people with a first stroke are under 65.","A leading cause of disability. Third leading cause of death in women, fifth in men, fourth overall. Someone has a stroke about every 40 seconds and someone dies from one every 4 minutes; about $36.5 billion in healthcare costs a year."]},{"s":"Stroke Risk Factors▾","a":"stroke-risk-factors","k":["Stroke Risk Factors▾","Hypertension — Especially uncontrolled or undiagnosed. Commonly leads to hemorrhagic stroke, often by way of an aneurysm that ruptures, or by weakening the arterial wall until it ruptures. More than doubles the risk of intracranial hemorrha…","Atherosclerosis and hyperlipidemia — Vessel damage leads to plaque formation and clots, in brain vessels as in the heart.","Tobacco use — Damages the endothelial lining of blood vessels.","Diabetes — Damages blood vessel walls, which can lead to plaque formation.","Oral contraceptives — Particularly estrogen and progesterone: increase plasma fibrinogen and coagulation factor activity.","Age (starred) — Advanced age: higher risk of stroke but a better chance of recovery, because brain atrophy leaves more room in the closed skull for swelling and bleeding.","Male sex — Males are at higher risk overall.","Sickle cell anemia — In crisis, blood cells clump and can lodge in brain vessels.","Atrial fibrillation — Quivering atria let blood pool and clot; the clot can travel to the brain. Often treated with anticoagulants such as warfarin, which bring their own bleeding risk."]},{"s":"Signs of a Stroke & the First Test▾","a":"signs-of-a-stroke-the-first-test","k":["Signs of a Stroke & the First Test▾","Clinical manifestations","Numbness or weakness of the face, arm or leg, usually on one side (asymmetrical weakness).","Sudden confusion; difficulty speaking or understanding speech; slurred speech (dysarthria).","Vision trouble in one or both eyes.","Trouble walking, balance problems, dizziness, lack of coordination (ataxia).","A sudden, severe headache with no known cause, particularly important in hemorrhagic stroke.","CT without contrast, done very quickly","Part of the stroke alert protocol (with attention to airway and breathing as needed); it drives the rest of the treatment plan.","Blood shows up right away as white; an ischemic stroke shows as a darkened area.","We must know ischemic vs hemorrhagic: never anticoagulate or give a thrombolytic to a hemorrhagic stroke. It makes the bleeding worse and could kill the client."]},{"s":"Ischemic Stroke▾","a":"ischemic-stroke","k":["Ischemic Stroke▾","An occlusion of a cerebral artery by a thrombus or an embolus; everything distal to the occlusion loses its blood supply.","Both types typically present the same way, based on which part of the brain isn't perfused, but the etiology guides treatment: atherosclerosis? a hypercoagulability problem? a thrombus or embolism elsewhere?","On CT, a darkened area that isn't prominent at first but becomes more noticeable if blood flow isn't restored.","A tiny vessel occlusion may go unnoticed (evidence of an old stroke is sometimes found on an unrelated CT). A larger vessel occlusion produces symptoms based on its location.","Neuro deficits start within about one minute of oxygen deprivation; without reestablished blood flow, the damage becomes irreversible.","Hemorrhagic conversion: after an ischemic stroke, blood from the peripheral circulation extravasates across a disrupted blood-brain barrier into the brain tissue once reperfusion is established, naturally or as a result of therapy. A hemorr…","Thrombotic — Arterial obstruction from thrombus formation. Atherosclerosis: vessel-lining damage leads to plaque that narrows or blocks the vessel; if the plaque ruptures, a clot forms. Hypercoagulable states: cancer, oral contraceptives, a…","Embolic — Look at the heart first: Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain. Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from po…","Mural thrombus: a clot in the ventricle (the image shows the left ventricle); a piece breaks off and travels through the aorta and carotids to the brain.","Atrial fibrillation: a left atrial thrombus from pooled blood. Usually arterial clots, on the left side of the heart.","Patent foramen ovale: a birth-defect hole in the septum between the atria lets a venous clot (such as a DVT piece) pass from the right atrium to the left atrium instead of going to the lungs as a PE; the left ventricle then sends it to the …","Valve infection, particularly the mitral valve: vegetation forms, and a piece breaks off as an embolus.","Mechanical valve replacement (more than a porcine valve): clots can form around the valve.","Carotid artery plaque can rupture, and the clot travels to the brain.","The lecture describes the mitral valve as \"between the right and left ventricle.\" It is kept out of the table as a likely slip; check the slide.","Assessment clues for embolic risk","Carotid bruit: a swishing sound on auscultation from turbulent flow through a vessel narrowed by a growing plaque.","ECG: in A-fib, little quivering lines replace the P waves (the atria aren't contracting properly). These clients are at higher risk for embolic stroke, especially if newly diagnosed and not yet anticoagulated long enough."]},{"s":"Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra▾","a":"time-is-tissue-the-penumbra","k":["Time Is Tissue: The Penumbra▾","Dead tissue (core)","Injured beyond repair.","Penumbra","The extended area of injured but salvageable tissue around the core.","Goal for ischemic stroke: treatment within 3 hours of symptom onset, to reestablish blood flow and save the penumbra. Once a stroke protocol is activated, the client goes to CT first (often bypassing a room), labs are drawn quickly and the …"]},{"s":"Hemorrhagic Stroke▾","a":"hemorrhagic-stroke","k":["Hemorrhagic Stroke▾","A ruptured vessel with bleeding within the brain tissue (the parenchyma).","Leading cause: untreated hypertension; over time the pressure weakens vessels until they rupture. Clients with aneurysms are also at increased risk.","About 38% mortality, within minutes to hours; in the brainstem, death can occur within seconds. Mortality depends on the size of the area affected.","Blood is very irritating to brain tissue, so inflammation causes edema; blood plus edema in the confined skull sharply increases ICP. The brain tissue can collapse on itself (herniation), which usually results in death.","Usually ages 30 to 60, younger than ischemic stroke clients. High morbidity and mortality, with serious disabilities.","Prognosis depends on","Age: older clients have brain atrophy and more room, so a better prognosis.","Location and size: the brainstem has a very poor prognosis.","How rapid the bleeding is, and whether it causes brain distortion or shift. A midline shift (a bleed pushing one side across the center line, seen on a CT report) means a very poor prognosis.","Presentation","The worst headache of their life.","A rapid change in level of consciousness.","With subdural, epidural or subarachnoid bleeds, the client presents like meningitis from meningeal irritation: nuchal rigidity and photophobia.","Meningeal signs (nuchal rigidity, photophobia) are covered in Neuro Infectious Diseases"]},{"s":"Other Intracranial Hemorrhages▾","a":"other-intracranial-hemorrhages","k":["Other Intracranial Hemorrhages▾","Bleeding in the layers covering the brain, named by location. A bleed in the brain tissue itself is a hemorrhagic stroke.","Epidural — Between the skull and the dura mater. — Usually with skull fractures (think trauma). Tend to be arterial, so even a less severe injury can mean a lot of blood loss.","Subdural — Below the dura. — Tend to be venous, e.g., injury to bridging veins. Severe injury can cause rapid decline, but venous blood may accumulate slowly: a client hits their head, seems fine, and symptoms appear later.","Subarachnoid — Between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, where the CSF lives. — Usually from an aneurysm or an AVM. Causes bloody CSF.","Treatment for epidural and subdural bleeds is to evacuate the collecting blood and relieve pressure on the brain. Blood bleeding into the brain tissue cannot be evacuated."]},{"s":"Subarachnoid Hemorrhage▾","a":"subarachnoid-hemorrhage","k":["Subarachnoid Hemorrhage▾","Diagnosis by lumbar puncture","Normal CSF is clear or slightly yellow. The first tube may have a little blood from needle trauma, but it should clear. With a subarachnoid bleed, the fluid stays bloody. A subarachnoid hemorrhage is identified by lumbar puncture about 12 h…","Bed rest and BP control — Avoid big BP fluctuations: keep it managed to prevent further bleeding, but high enough to perfuse the brain. The nurse is often given very specific BP parameters.","Surgery — If an aneurysm is the culprit, surgical intervention (clipping or coiling), usually done ASAP.","Anticonvulsant — Blood irritates the brain, so seizure risk rises.","Calcium channel blocker — After clipping or coiling, to help treat the symptoms of the subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Triple H — Keeps the brain perfused: pressors to keep BP high enough, colloids and a hypervolemic state, and some expected hemodilution.","The lecture describes the calcium channel blocker as working \"by narrowing and slowing that blood flow.\" It is kept out of the table as stated; check the slide for the intended mechanism."]},{"s":"Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling▾","a":"berry-aneurysms-avms-clipping-coiling","k":["Berry Aneurysms, AVMs, Clipping & Coiling▾","Berry aneurysm — The most common aneurysm that ruptures in the brain: little berries hanging off the artery that rupture when big enough.","AVM (arteriovenous malformation) — Normally arteries divide into arterioles, capillaries, venules, then veins. In an AVM the junction is a jumbled clump, so high-pressure arterial blood goes directly into the vein without slowing through ca…","Clipping — A clip is placed around the aneurysm to block blood flow into it.","Coiling (endovascular embolization) — A catheter inserted in the groin is threaded into the aneurysm, and detachable platinum coils reduce blood flow into the sac, lowering the chance of rupture.","The lecture also says \"berry aneurysms are often located in the veins,\" which conflicts with the same slide (\"hanging off the artery\") and with Week 1. It is kept out of the table; check the slide.","Berry aneurysms were first covered in Aneurysms (Week 1)"]},{"s":"BE FAST & Last Known Normal▾","a":"be-fast-last-known-normal","k":["BE FAST & Last Known Normal▾","B: Balance — Headache, dizziness, loss of balance, trouble walking; falls, especially in the elderly.","E: Eyes — Sudden vision loss in one or both eyes; unequal pupils.","F: Face — Facial asymmetry: have them smile. Differentiate from Bell's palsy.","A: Arm — Weakness: both arms held up perpendicular to the body; does one drift?","S: Speech — Difficulty speaking or slurred words.","T: Time — Call 911, get to the hospital, activate a stroke alert.","Find out exactly when symptoms started: the \"last known normal.\" Treatment must happen within a certain time frame."]},{"s":"Stroke Treatment: First Steps▾","a":"stroke-treatment-first-steps","k":["Stroke Treatment: First Steps▾","ABCs: can they maintain their airway, are they breathing, is there a pulse? A GCS below 8 raises concern about airway protection, so the client is intubated. Stroke clients may also have decreased respiratory drive.","Stroke alert / protocol: labs, CT, the neuro team, and a blood sugar check, because hypoglycemia can mimic a stroke.","CT scan without contrast, done first to exclude hemorrhagic stroke: fast, and best in the acute stage. An MRI gives better images later, but it is slower and less available, so it isn't the first choice in an emergency.","Hemorrhagic stroke","Reverse anticoagulation: is there a reversal agent or antidote for the client's medications?","Manage hypertension, the likely cause.","Manage ICP: elevate the head of the bed, give antipyretics for fever, keep the head in a neutral position.","Ischemic stroke","Thrombolytics (clot busters) to open the occlusion and protect and reperfuse the penumbra.","Mechanical thrombectomy: a surgeon removes the clot manually (also called a penumbra procedure)."]},{"s":"Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke▾","a":"alteplase-tpa-for-ischemic-stroke","k":["Alteplase (tPA) for Ischemic Stroke▾","Heparin and antiplatelets don't dissolve a clot; they only prevent one from forming. Fibrinolytics actually break up clots.","Alteplase (tPA, tissue plasminogen activator), the most commonly used fibrinolytic, converts plasminogen to plasmin; plasmin digests the fibrin.","Used in severe thrombotic events: acute MI, PE, ischemic stroke, occasionally femoral artery clots or a clotted central line. Given IV.","Monitor BP — It can cause hypotension.","Monitor HR and rhythm — Arrhythmias can develop with reperfusion.","Bleeding risk — Increased risk of intracranial bleeding. Don't give it to clients at high risk for bleeding (such as a previous hemorrhagic stroke or a GI bleed).","CT first — Always rule out hemorrhagic stroke before giving it.","With heparin — Often given with heparin to prevent new clots from forming.","Antidote — Aminocaproic acid, an antithrombolytic: blocks plasminogen binding and activation to plasmin, reducing fibrinolysis to stop bleeding. Give it only if the hemorrhage is life-threatening.","Alteplase was first covered for acute MI in Acute MI Pharmacology (Week 1)"]},{"s":"Consequences of Stroke▾","a":"consequences-of-stroke","k":["Consequences of Stroke▾","Left side of the brain affected","Difficulty with communication (verbal and written) and with more analytical activities.","Right side of the brain affected","Difficulty with things like imagination, music and art.","Dysphagia — A swallow evaluation comes before giving food or advancing a diet. Speech pathology does a barium swallow study under real-time x-ray with different thicknesses of food and liquid, then recommends a diet: regular, thickened liqu…","Motor and sensory deficits — Neglect of body parts no longer getting sensory input; poor coordination (fall risk); loss of muscle groups, which atrophy over time.","Flaccidity → spasticity → contractures — Flaccid (limp, weak) muscles on the contralateral side become spastic (painful), then contractures (permanently shortened muscles, joints stuck in abnormal positions), typically within the first 6 we…","Homonymous hemianopia — Contralateral field blindness: loss (or blurring) of up to half the vision, on the same side of both visual fields.","Aphasia — The global term for affected language: dysphasia (speech), receptive aphasia (understanding), reading and writing problems; one or all.","Depression — Very common, from significant disabilities and loss of independence.","Memory problems — Even well-known things like family members' names.","Behavior changes — Increased emotional response (upset, angry or crying more easily); slower reaction times; hesitance or caution; confusion or disorientation; apathy.","The lecture also says clients \"can underestimate their own abilities\" and calls it a big safety concern, alongside slower reaction times (fall risk). That is kept as stated; check the slide wording."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 4","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics","The Three Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It's a map of Week 4's three topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate; it pulls the whole week…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-the-three-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to the Three Topics▾","The stroke topic's four voiceovers (TIA, stroke and hemorrhage parts 1 and 2, and stroke treatment) share one page.","The Nerves Break Down — Topic 1, ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome (the ALS sections first, then the GBS sections) — Both take away muscle strength until the breathing muscles are in danger. ALS kills the upper and lower motor neurons and doesn…","Infection Around and Inside the Brain — Topic 2, Neuro Infectious Diseases (meningitis, then encephalitis, then brain abscess) — Bacteria or viruses reach the meninges, the brain tissue, or a pocket of pus. Inflammation and swelling in a cl…","The Blood Supply Fails — Topic 3, TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage (TIA, then stroke and ischemic stroke, then hemorrhagic stroke and bleeds, then treatment and consequences) — A blocked vessel (TIA, ischemic stroke) or a ruptured one (hemorrhagic …","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order. Read neuro infections before the hemorrhagic stroke sections (the meningeal signs and meningeal layers are taught there first), and the TIA sections before stroke."]},{"s":"The Three Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-three-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Three Topics at a Glance▾","1 — ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome — ALS: progressive, fatal upper and lower motor neuron degeneration, linked to excess glutamate; death from respiratory failure about 3 years after diagnosis; riluzole (hepatotoxic) buys about 6 months to a…","2 — Neuro Infectious Diseases — Meningitis: S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis by droplets; fever, headache, nuchal rigidity, photophobia, Kernig and Brudzinski, a non-blanching rash with meningococcus; bacterial kills in hours, so IV antibi…","3 — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage — TIA: under 24 hours, no infarction, but a warning (highest stroke risk in the first month). Stroke: 87% ischemic (thrombotic or embolic; save the penumbra within 3 hours with alteplase or thrombectomy) and 13%…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content. Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why. Section names are in quotation marks.","The meninges — Neuro Infectious Diseases: \"Meningitis: What It Is & What Causes It\" (pia, arachnoid and dura mater; CSF in the subarachnoid space) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: \"Other Intracranial Hemorrhages\" names each bleed by which layer …","Meningeal signs — Neuro Infectious Diseases: \"Meningitis: Clinical Manifestations\" (nuchal rigidity, photophobia, Kernig and Brudzinski) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: subdural, epidural and subarachnoid bleeds present like meningitis.","The closed skull and ICP — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: \"Hemorrhagic Stroke\" (blood plus edema in a confined space → herniation) — Neuro Infectious Diseases: meningitis swelling raises ICP; a brain abscess is drained to lower the pressure. Str…","Headache — Neuro Infectious Diseases: the meningitis triad — Encephalitis and brain abscess; stroke's sudden, severe headache; hemorrhagic stroke's \"worst headache of their life.\"","Seizures and anticonvulsants — Neuro Infectious Diseases: seizures in about 30% of meningitis; anti-seizure drugs in the acute phase of meningitis and encephalitis — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: an anticonvulsant after a subarachnoid hemorrhag…","Breathing failure — ALS & GBS: ALS death from respiratory failure; GBS ascending to the breathing muscles (ventilator, sometimes a tracheostomy) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: ABCs first, intubate at a GCS below 8, decreased respiratory drive.","Swallowing and aspiration — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: \"Key Terms\" (dysphagia with a G, NPO) and \"Consequences of Stroke\" (the barium swallow study) — ALS & GBS: difficulty chewing and swallowing in ALS; aspiration risk in GBS.","Falls — ALS & GBS: GBS uncoordinated movements and decreased sensation (high fall risk) — ALS: tripping and falling. Stroke: BE FAST balance (falls in the elderly), poor coordination, slower reaction times.","Vision changes — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: carotid and vertebrobasilar TIA signs, BE FAST eyes, homonymous hemianopia — ALS & GBS: blurred vision as an early GBS sign. Neuro Infectious Diseases: photophobia; a temporal lobe abscess causes v…","Steroids — ALS & GBS: \"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Treatment & Prognosis\" (high dose, titrated down) — Neuro Infectious Diseases: steroids reduce meningitis inflammation.","Demyelination — ALS & GBS: \"Guillain-Barré Syndrome: What It Is\" (peripheral myelin) — ALS: motor neuron death leads to demyelination and sclerosis. Week 3's Multiple Sclerosis is the CNS version.","Aneurysms and alteplase — Week 1: Aneurysms (berry aneurysms) and Acute MI Pharmacology (alteplase) — TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage: berry aneurysms rupture into subarachnoid bleeds; alteplase busts the clot in ischemic stroke, with the same ble…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the three pages, condensed to a phrase, one row per topic. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation. Click through to a topic for the details behind any…","ALS & Guillain-Barré Syndrome — Both: watch the breathing muscles. Riluzole is hepatotoxic: watch liver function. GBS: rapid decline over 24 hours can mean a ventilator; high fall risk; decreased sensation hides injuries; aspiration risk.","Neuro Infectious Diseases — Bacterial meningitis: antibiotics as soon as possible, never held (it kills within hours). A non-blanching petechial rash means meningococcus. Septic emboli can cost fingers or limbs. Very communicable by droplet…","TIA, Stroke & Hemorrhage — After a TIA, go to the ED for any new episode. Non-contrast CT before any anticoagulant or thrombolytic: never give them to a hemorrhagic stroke. Treat ischemic stroke within 3 hours; get the last known normal. GC…","Patterns You'll Notice Across These Rules","\"The skull is a closed box.\" Infection swelling, an abscess and a bleed all fight for the same fixed space, so ICP and herniation are the shared danger, and the older, atrophied brain tolerates it best.","\"Time decides the outcome.\" Bacterial meningitis kills in hours, the penumbra dies without reperfusion within 3 hours, and rapid GBS can need a ventilator within a day.","\"Rule out the bleed first.\" The non-contrast CT comes before alteplase, anticoagulants and the rest of the stroke plan, because the right treatment for a clot can kill a client who is bleeding.","\"Watch the airway, the breathing and the swallowing.\" ALS and GBS weaken the breathing and swallowing muscles; stroke clients may lose their airway or their swallow, so they're intubated below GCS 8 and kept NPO until the swallow study.","\"Cover the worst cause until it's ruled out.\" Acyclovir is given with antibiotics until the CSF rules out a viral cause, and every suspected stroke is treated as an emergency even though some turn out to be TIAs or Bell's palsy."]}]}];
